  <page>
    <title>King Solomon's Mines (film)</title>
    <id>80952</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38854113</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-09T01:42:52Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Lady Aleena</username>
        <id>239144</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Changed category</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:King_Solomon's_mines_1985_movie.jpg|thumb|1985 version movie poster]]
'''''King Solomon's Mines''''' refers to at least three films which tell the story of an adventurer who helps a woman look for her lost husband/father in [[Africa]], loosely based on the novel ''[[King Solomon's Mines]]'' (1885) by [[H. Rider Haggard]].


==1937 film==
''King Solomon's Mines'' first received a film treatment in [[1937]], starring [[Paul Robeson]], [[Cedric Hardwicke]], [[Anna Lee]] and [[Roland Young]].  It was adapted by [[Charles Bennett (screenwriter)|Charles Bennett]] (uncredited), [[Michael Hogan]], [[Roland Pertwee]] [[A.R. Rawlinson]] (uncredited) and [[Ralph Spence]] (uncredited).  It was directed by [[Robert Stevenson (director)|Robert Stevenson]].

==1950 film==
In [[1950]], the movie was adapted by [[Helen Deutsch]] from the novel.  It stars [[Deborah Kerr]], [[Stewart Granger]] and [[Richard Carlson]].  It was directed by [[Compton Bennett]] and [[Andrew Marton]]. It won [[Academy Awards]] for [[Academy Award for Best Cinematography|Best Cinematography, Color]] and [[Academy Award for Film Editing|Best Film Editing]] and was nominated for [[Academy Award for Best Picture|Best Picture]].

==1985 film==
It was [[remake|remade]] in [[1985]], starring [[Richard Chamberlain]], [[Sharon Stone]], [[Herbert Lom]] and [[John Rhys-Davies]].  It was adapted by [[Gene Quintano]] and [[James R. Silke]] and directed by [[J. Lee Thompson]].

==2004 TV film==
King Solomon's Mines was also remade as a TV mini series in 2004. It starred [[Patrick Swayze]] as Allan Quatermain and also starred [[Alison Doody]].

==External links==
*{{imdb title | id=0029081 | title=King Solomon's Mines (1937)}}
*[http://www.archive.org/details/king_solomans_mine Free download of 1937 version] at the [[Internet Archive]]
