[[Image:Luffa_sponge.png|thumb|left|250px|A luffa sponge whose coarse texture helps with [[skin]] polishing.]]

The '''luffas''' (from [[Arabic language|Arabic]] &amp;#1604;&amp;#1608;&amp;#1601;; also spelled '''loofah''' or '''loofa''') are tropical and subtropical annual [[vine]]s comprising the genus '''''Luffa'''''.  The fruit of at least two species, ''L. acutangula'' and ''L. aegyptiaca'', is grown to be harvested before maturity and eaten as a [[vegetable]], sometimes called '''Chinese okra''', popular in [[Asia]] and [[Africa]].  ''L. acutangula'' is also known as ''Ridge Gourd'' and is called ''jhingey'' in [[Bengali]], ''turai'' in [[Hindi]]. The fruit of ''L. aegyptiaca'' may also be allowed to mature and used as a bath or kitchen [[sponge]] after being processed to remove everything but the network of [[xylem]]s. This version is called ''dhundul'' in [[Bengali]], ''ghiya tori'' or ''nerua'' in [[Hindi]]. &quot;peerkankai&quot; in [[Tamil language|Tamil]].

''Luffa'' species are used as food plants by the [[larva]]e of some [[Lepidoptera]] species including ''[[Hypercompe|Hypercompe albicornis]]''.

== External links ==

*[http://www.plantnames.unimelb.edu.au/Sorting/Luffa.html Multilingual taxonomic information at the University of Melbourne]

*[http://www.floridata.com/ref/l/luff_aeg.cfm Luffa aegyptiaca at Floridata]

Also see [[Gourd]].

