  <page>
    <title>Evadne</title>
    <id>81122</id>
    <revision>
      <id>15972704</id>
      <timestamp>2004-08-28T23:19:26Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Bryan Derksen</username>
        <id>66</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>[[Category:Greek mythological people]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], there were two people named '''Evadne'''.

#A daughter of [[Poseidon]] and mother of [[Iamus]] by [[Apollo (god)|Apollo]].  She was ashamed of her pregnancy and exposed the child to the elements.  He survived and founded the [[Iamos]], a family of priests from [[Olympia, Greece|Olympia]].
#A daughter of [[Iphis]] and wife of [[Capaneus]], with whom she gave birth to [[Sthenelus]].  Her husband was killed by a lightning bolt at the [[Seven Against Thebes|siege]] of [[Thebes (Greece)|Thebes]] and she threw herself on his funeral pyre and died.  ([[Virgil]] VI, 447.)

Alternative: '''Euadne'''

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Iamus</title>
    <id>81123</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34106988</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-06T13:33:46Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Krashlandon</username>
        <id>750649</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], '''Iamus''' was the son of [[Evadne]], a daughter of [[Poseidon]], sired by [[Apollo (god)|Apollo]].

Shamed by her pregnancy, Evadne exposed the child to the elements. He survived and, granted the gift of prophecy by Apollo, founded the [[Iamos]], a family of priests from [[Olympia, Greece|Olympia]].

{{Greek-myth-stub}} 

==Reference==
[http://www.pantheon.org/articles/i/iamus.html Iamus] at the Encyclopedia Mythica Web site
[[Category:Greek mythological people]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Capaneus</title>
    <id>81124</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34799767</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-11T20:48:03Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Arabani</username>
        <id>208967</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>grammar fixes</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], '''Capaneus''' was a son of [[Hipponous]] and husband of [[Evadne]], with whom he fathered [[Sthenelus]].

According to the legend, Capaneus had immense strength and body size and was an outstanding warrior. He was also notorious for his arrogance. He stood just at the wall of Thebes at the [[Seven Against Thebes|siege]] of [[Thebes (Greece)|Thebes]] and shouted that Zeus himself could not stop him from invading it. Zeus struck and killed Capaneus with a thunderbolt, and Evadne threw herself on her husband's funeral pyre and died.  His story was told by [[Aeschylus]], [[Euripides]], and the Roman poet [[Statius]]. 

In [[the Divine Comedy]], Dante sees Capaneus in the seventh circle of Hell. Along with the other blasphemers, or those &quot;violent against God&quot;, Capaneus is condemned to lie supine on a plain of burning sand while fire rains down on him. He continues to curse the deity (whom, being a pagan, he addresses as &quot;Jove&quot; aka Jupiter) despite the ever harsher pains he thus inflicts upon himself, so that God &quot;thereby should not have glad vengeance.&quot;

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]
[[Category:Characters in the Divine Comedy]]

[[bg:Капаней]]
[[ru:Капаней]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Hipponous</title>
    <id>81125</id>
    <revision>
      <id>20082780</id>
      <timestamp>2005-08-02T00:29:57Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>GeeJo</username>
        <id>296804</id>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], '''Hipponous''' referred to several people:

*One was the father of [[Capaneus]] and [[Periboea]] with [[Astymone]].
*Another was one of the fifty sons of [[Priam]].
*Another was the last [[Troy|Trojan]] who [[Achilles]] killed before his death.
*The name was sometimes used in reference to [[Bellerophon]].

{{Greek-myth-stub}}

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]
[[Category:People who fought in the Trojan War]]</text>
