  <page>
    <title>Pedro del Valle</title>
    <id>81141</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39299144</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-12T04:37:05Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Looper5920</username>
        <id>433748</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* External link */ add USNA category</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Lieutenant General Pedro Augusto del Valle''' ([[August 28]], [[1893]] &amp;ndash; [[April 28]], [[1978]]) in [[San Juan, Puerto Rico]]) was the first Hispanic in the history of the [[United States Marine Corps]] to reach the rank of [[lieutenant general]] (three star general).
{| border=1 align=right cellpadding=4 cellspacing=0 width=180 style=&quot;margin: 0.5em 0 1em 1em; background: white; border: 2px red solid; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 95%;&quot;
| align=center colspan=&quot;2&quot; |
{| style=&quot;background:white;&quot; border=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;2&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot;
|[[image:USAF.insignia.o9.collar.wag.png|50px|center]]
|-
|- align=center
| [[Image:Pedro del Valle.jpg|150px]]
|
|-align=center
|&lt;center&gt;'''Lt. Gen. Pedro del Valle'''&lt;/center&gt;
|
|-align=center
|&lt;center&gt;'''(1893-1978)'''&lt;/center&gt;
|
|- align=center 
| &lt;center&gt;[[Image:USMarineCorps.png|80px]]&lt;/center&gt; 
|
|-align=center
|  &lt;center&gt;[[Image:Us_legion_of_merit_legionnaire.png|105px|center]]&lt;/center&gt;
|-
| &lt;center&gt;'''Legion of Merit medal'''&lt;/center&gt;
|
|- align=center
|}
|-
|}
Del Valle was born in Puerto Rico when the island was still under Spanish colonial rule.  His father was Dr. Francesco del Valle, a surgeon who had served as mayor of San Juan.  In [[1900]], two years after the [[Spanish-American War]], the del Valle family moved to Maryland where they became U.S. citizens (The [[Jones Act]] of 1917 later gave United States Citizenship to all Puerto Ricans born in the island). He received his primary and secondary education in Maryland.  Upon graduating high school del Valle was accepted in the [[United States Naval Academy|U.S. Naval Academy]] in [[Annapolis, Maryland]].  Del Valle graduated from the academy in June [[1915]] and was commissioned a [[second lieutenant]] of Marines on [[June 5]], 1915. 

Pedro del Valle helped the [[United States Marine Corps|Marine Corps]] in the capture of [[Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic]] in [[1916]] and was awarded his first [[Legion of Merit]].  Del Valle commanded the Marine detachment on board the [[USS Texas (BB-35)|USS ''Texas'']] (BB-35) in the North Atlantic during [[World War I]].  In [[1919]], he participated in the surrender of the [[Germany|German]] [[High Seas Fleet]].

In [[1926]], del Valle served with the [[Gendarmerie]] of [[Haiti]] for three years and during that time he also became active in the war against [[Augusto Sandino]] in [[Nicaragua]].  In [[1929]] he returned to the [[United States|U.S.]], he attended the Field Officers Course at the Marine Corps School in [[Marine Corps Base Quantico|MCB Quantico]], [[Virginia]].  

In [[1931]], Brigadier General [[Randolph C. Berkeley]] appointed del Valle to the &quot;Landing Operations Text Board&quot; in Quantico, the first organizational step taken by the Marines to develop a working doctrine for [[amphibious assault]].  He worked as an intelligence officer in [[Havana]], [[Cuba]] in [[1933]] under Admiral Charles Freeman, following the Cuban Sergeant's Reevolt.  From [[1935]]&amp;ndash;[[1937|37]], del Valle was Assistant Naval Attache, attached to the [[American Embassy]] in [[Rome, Italy]].  While on duty, del Valle paticipated as an observer with the [[Italian Army|Italian Forces]] during the [[Second Italo-Abyssinian War]].  In [[1939]], he was ordered to attend the [[Army War College]] in [[Washington, D.C.]] and after graduating was named Executive Officer of the Division of Plans and Policies. 

On [[March]] [[1941]], del Valle became the [[Commanding Officer]] of the [[U.S. 11th Marine Regiment|11th Marines]], ([[artillery]]).  Upon the outbreak of [[World War II]], del Valle led his [[regiment]] and participated in the [[Battle of Guadalcanal|seizure and defense of Guadalcanal]] as part of the [[U.S. 1st Marine Division|First Marine Division]]. 
In [[1943]], he served as Commander of Marine Forces overseeing [[Guadalcanal]], [[Tulagi]], [[Russell Island|Russell]] and [[Florida Island]]s.

On [[April 1]], [[1944]], del Valle, as Commanding General of the Third Corps Artillery, [[U.S. III Amphibious Corps|Third Amphibious Corps]], took part in the [[Battle of Guam|Guam operation]] and was awarded a Gold Star in lieu of a second Legion of Merit.  He then became the Commanding General of the First Marine Division and was awarded a [[Distinguished Service Medal (United States)|Distinguished Service Medal]] for his leadership in the reorganization of [[Okinawa]].     

After WWII ended, del Valle was ordered back to Headquarters Marine Corps, where he was named Inspector General, position which he held until he retired on [[January 1]], [[1948]].  Del Valle worked as as a representative of [[ITT]] in the company's office in [[Cairo]], [[Egypt]].  After sometime with the company he was named president of ITT for all [[South America]] in [[Buenos Aires]], [[Argentina]] a position that he held until [[1951]].

Believing that the United States was in danger of a [[communist]] threat, del Valle tried to convince the [[CIA]] and the Pentagon to form a vigilante minuteman group.  He also believed that the CIA should operate behind [[Russia|Russian]] and [[China|Chinese]] lines.  After his ideas were turned down, he decided to form his group.  In [[1953]], del Valle met with Lt. Col. John H. Hoffman (USMC), Lt. Col. Eugene Cowles Poneroy, Brigadier General Bonner Fellers and Major General Claire Chennault (USAF) and formed the &quot;Defenders of the American Constitution&quot; (DAC).  The idea behind the group was to organize the citizens in each state as vigilantes against sabotage and other forms of treason. Then link them up in some national headquarters.  

Lieutenant General Pedro del Valle died on [[April 28]], 1978 in Annapolis, Maryland. After del Valle’s death at age 85  the DAC ceased to exist. 


Among Lieutenant General Pedro del Valle's decorations and medals were the following:  

* [[Distinguished Service Medal (United States)|Distinguished Service Medal]],&lt;!--presumably [[Navy Distinguished Service Medal]]--&gt; 
* [[Legion of Merit]] with a Gold Star, 
* [[Navy and Marine Corps Medal]], Ethiopia 1936&amp;ndash;36, 
* [[Presidential Unit Citation (US)|Presidential Unit Citation]], Guadalcanal 1942, Okinawa 1945, 
* [[Expeditionary Medal]] with Bronze Star, Haiti 1916, 
* [[Dominican Campaign Medal]], Dominican Republic 1916, 
* [[World War I Victory Medal]] 1918, 
* [[Second Nicaraguan Campaign Medal]], Nicaragua 1930, 
* [[American Defense Service Medal]], 
* [[Asiatic-Pacific Campaign Medal]] with five Bronze Stars, 
* [[American Campaign Medal]], 
* [[World War II Victory Medal]], 
* [[Order of the Crown of Italy]], Italy 1936, 
* [[East African Medal]], 
* [[Colonial Order of the Star of Italy]], 
* [[Italian Bronze Medal]] for Military Valor, 
* [[Cuban Naval Order of Merit]] second class, Cuba 1938, 
* [[Ecuadorian Decoration of Abdon Calderon Star]] first class with Diploma, Ecuador 1942.

==See also==
*[[List of famous Puerto Ricans#Military|List of famous Puerto Ricans - Military]]

==External link==
