  <page>
    <title>Cross-platform</title>
    <id>81193</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42078481</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T17:56:43Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>83.209.23.201</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>I somehow turned libraries into librares... fixed</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{confusing}}

__NOTOC__
A '''cross-platform''' (or '''platform independent''') [[software]] application or [[hardware]] device containing [[firmware]] works on more than just one [[system platform]] (e.g. [[Linux]]/[[Unix]], [[Microsoft Windows|Windows]], [[Apple Macintosh|Macintosh]]). A more rigid interpretation of cross-platform is working on &quot;every&quot; platform meaning on most or on any important platform, only excluding special cases.

[[Programming languages]] used to write these software or firmware are per definition platform independent so the dependency is given through the use of the tools (compilers and interpreters) and the environment (libraries and [[operating system|OS]] functionality). In the early days of software development the availability of the tools determined if a programming language was widely usable. These days one can assume that the tools for most languages are available on any platform. Similar but not always libraries are available anywhere (e.g. [[OpenGL]]).

What hasn't changed so far is the dependency on the OS functionality. On contrary this functionality, e.g. disk and file access, has been greatly extended through the introduction of a [[graphical user interface]] (GUI) using a [[Widget_(computing)|widget set]], the implementation of [[network]] access services, etc. The access to any of these functionality is defined through platform specific [[API|Application Programming Interface]]s (APIs) which are used within platform specific [[framework]]s. A sample for a platform specific framework is the [[MFC|Microsoft Foundation Classes]].

With the existence of frameworks which contains (hides) the platform specific APIs, the possibility of hiding the platform specific functionality comes into mind. In other words with cross-platform frameworks cross-platform software becomes a reality. One of the first such a framework is the [[Java|Java programming environment]]. Some of these frameworks are only partially cross-platform, since they implement only some of the functionality cross-platform, only on a limited range of platforms or in a framework distinct way. Therefore, even if frameworks are to some extent cross-platform, each might not be well suited to build cross-platform software.

==Platform independent software==
Software code not using any platform specific feature is itself platform independent. E.g. a quicksort procedure can be used on any platform as long as special care is taken about input and output. The need for cross-platform doesn't arise with such platform independent code so it's always a good idea to separate it from the rest.

==Web applications==
[[Web application]]s are normally considered cross-platform by definition since they are interpreted by a web browser and do not use platform-specific features directly. Unfortunately, not all browsers behave equally so some platform or browser-specific code is needed. But as long as web applications limit their functionality to the general available, cross-platform is a rather minor issue. Besides there exists all kinds of quirks and hacks to work around such problems albeit they might not be correct in the future.

A web page containing content not renderable by all browsers such as [[Macromedia Flash]] presentations may still be sort of cross-platform if allowances are made for people without the special plugins. For example, images to be shown in place of rich content or a downloadable [[MPEG-2]] version of animated material.

Cross-platform web accessibility requires as much an understanding of users as of technical standards if a page is to be accessible on platforms as varied as [[screen reader]]s, [[Braille]]-based web browsers, and small devices like [[mobile phone]]s and [[Personal digital assistant|PDAs]].

A very good guide for coding web applications is [http://de.selfhtml.org/ SelfHTML] albeit it is only available in German.

==Ordinary applications==
Ordinary applications are the binary executables a user starts on his platform. These binaries of course run on a specific processor using the features of a certain OS system and handle all input/output through a platform specific GUI. So these binaries are in every respect platform specific. To create such binaries true cross-platform, a framework which takes care and hides of all these platform dependencies has to be used. A sample for a though out cross-platform framework is [[wxWidgets]].

==Cross-platform guidelines==
It is not easy to code these binary executables cross-platform since there are many small and some complex differences between platforms which have to be carefully taken care of. Therefore for cross-platform development cross-platform guidelines are needed. A guide for well-designed cross-platform development is [[wyoGuide]].

==Disadvantages and problems with Cross-platform development==

* Special features of a OS/CPU cannot be used, a subset available on all systems must be utilized
** Mostly slower than non-portable software, some features cannot be programmed
* Takes more time for programming
* Takes much more time for testing
* All systems are needed for testing

[[Category:Computing platforms]]

[[ca:Multiplataforma]]
[[de:Plattformunabhängigkeit]]
[[es:Multiplataforma]]
[[eo:Multesistema]]
[[fr:Multi-plateforme]]
[[it:Multipiattaforma]]
[[nl:Multiplatform]]
[[ru:Кроссплатформенное программное обеспечение]]
[[zh:跨平台]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
