      <comment>/* Rock Climbing and Hill Walking */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Glendalough_monastic_site.jpg|thumb|200px|Ancient church at Glendalough monastic site]]
[[Image:Irl_1sh_airmail.jpg|thumb|200px|1 shilling value Irish airmail stamp with ''Vox Hibernia'' flying over Glendalough]]
[[Image:Glendalough, Ireland.jpg|thumb|200px|Glendalough valley]]
[[Image:Glendalough round tower.jpg|thumb|200px|Round tower at Glendalough]]

'''Glendalough''' is a village located at the site of an ancient [[monastery]] located in [[County Wicklow]], [[Ireland]]. It was founded in the [[6th century]] by [[Kevin|St. Kevin]], a [[hermit]] [[priest]], and destroyed in [[1398]] by English troops.

== St. Kevin's Monastic Site ==

The location was originally sought out as a peaceful retreat by St. Kevin because of its remoteness and serenity, but several [[disciple]]s who wished to follow his teachings built a temporary [[hamlet (UK place)|hamlet]] of [[church]]es, [[chapel]]s and living quarters in the valley below the site of his [[hovel]]. This soon grew and eventually, at the height of its popularity, was one of the main [[religious]] universities in Ireland.

The name Glendalough comes from the [[Irish_language|Irish]] ''Gleann Dá Locha'' meaning the glen (valley) of two [[lake]]s. The monastery site is located between two lakes (Lower Lake and Upper Lake) and is one of the most popular [[tourist]] sites in Wicklow.

The monastic site includes a 33 metres tall [[round tower]] and St. Kevin's Cross, a [[Celtic cross|Celtic]] [[High cross]]. The round tower was built during the era of the [[viking]] invasions in Ireland (up to and around AD1066), in order to protect the religious [[relic]]s, books and [[chalice]]s used around the monastery at the time.

== Mining ==

At the west end of the Upper Lake lie the ruins of an abandoned miners' village that is, normally, accessible only by foot. [[Mining]], primarily for [[lead]], took place here from [[1850]] until about [[1875]] but the mines in the valley of Glendalough were smaller and less important than those in [[Glendasan Valley]], that are separated by [[Camaderry Mountain]]. In 1859 the Glendasan and Glendalough mines were connected with each other by a series of [[adit]]s, now flooded, through the mountain. This made it easier to transport ore to Glendalough and process it there.

An exhibition, '''The Mining Heritage of Wicklow,''' was displayed at the County Buildings, Wicklow Town, in September 2003, in association with Mining Heritage Trust of Ireland and [[County Wicklow | Wicklow County]] Council Heritage Office. The exhibition explored the mining heritage of Wicklow and two panels [http://www.gsi.ie/workgsi/heritage/exhibitions/wicklowexhib/glendalough1.htm] and [http://www.gsi.ie/workgsi/heritage/exhibitions/wicklowexhib/glendalough2.htm] were devoted to mining in Glendalough.

== Rock Climbing and Hill Walking ==

Glendalough's [[granite]] [[cliff|cliffs]], above the north-western end of the Upper Lake, are a popular [[climbing|rock-climbing]] location. The [[boulder|boulders]] below the cliffs are excellent for [[bouldering]].

There are many good local walks, of varying difficulty, to be made around Glendalough and a [http://www.wicklownationalpark.ie/pages/walkingtrails.php Glendalough Walking Trails] map is available from the [http://www.wicklownationalpark.ie/index.php Wicklow Mountains National Park] web site. The [[Wicklow Way]], a long distance way-marked [[Trail | walking trail]], passes through Glendalough on its way from [[Rathfarnham]] in the north to its southerly point of [[Clonegal]] in [[County Carlow]].

==See also==
*[[List of climbing_areas#Ireland|List of climbing areas in Ireland]]

