  <page>
    <title>Pietro d'Abano</title>
    <id>81272</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41220276</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-25T21:48:38Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Cuchullain</username>
        <id>196153</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>moved info from Heptameron</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{cleanup-date|December 2005}}
'''Pietro d'Abano''' ([[1246]] or [[1250]]?&amp;#8209;[[1316]]), also known as '''Petrus de Apono''' or '''Aponensis''', was an [[Italy|Italian]] [[physics|physician]], [[philosophy|philosopher]], and [[astrologer]]. He was born in 1250, or, according to others, in 1246, in the Italian town from which he takes his name, now [[Abano Terme]].  

After studying medicine and philosophy at [[Paris]] he settled at [[Padua]], where he quickly gained a great reputation as a physician, and set out to gratify his avarice by refusing to visit patients except for an exorbitant fee. Perhaps this, as well as his meddling with [[astrology]], caused him to be charged with practising [[magic]], the specific accusations being that he got back, by the aid of the [[devil]], all the money he paid away, and that he possessed the [[philosopher's stone]]. He was twice brought to trial by the [[Inquisition]]; on the first occasion he was acquitted, and he died before the second trial was completed. He was found guilty, however, and his body was ordered to be exhumed and burned; but a friend had secretly removed it, and the Inquisition had therefore to content itself with the public proclamation of its sentence and the burning of Abano in [[effigy]]. In his writings he expounds and advocates the medical and philosophical systems of [[Averroes]] and other [[Arabian]] writers. His best known works are the &lt;cite&gt;Conciliator differentiarum quae inter philosophos et medicos versantur&lt;/cite&gt; (Mantua, 1472; Venice, 1476), and &lt;cite&gt;De venenis eorumque remediis&lt;/cite&gt; (1472), of which a [[French language|French]] translation was published at [[Lyons]] in 1593.

Abano also wrote a [[grimoire]] called the ''Heptameron'', a concise book of ritual magical rites concerned with conjuring specific angels for the seven days of the week, whence the title. It should not be confused with the ''[[Heptameron]]'' of [[Marguerite of Navarre]].

{{Wikisource1911Enc|Abano, Pietro D}}
{{1911}}

