      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">&lt;div style=&quot;float:right; margin: 0 0 1em 1em;&quot;&gt;[[Image:Washington County Alabama.png]]&lt;/div&gt;
'''Washington County''' is a [[county]] of the [[U.S. state|State]] of [[Alabama]]. Its name is in honor of [[George Washington]], first [[President of the United States of America]].  As of [[2000]] the population was 18,097.  Its [[county seat]] is [[Chatom, Alabama|Chatom]]. Washington County is a prohibition or entirely [[dry county]].

== History ==
*Washington County was established on [[June 4]], [[1800]].
*The county was declared a disaster area in September [[1979]] due to damage from [[Hurricane Frederic]].

== Geography ==
According to the [[United States Census Bureau|U.S. Census Bureau]], the county has a total area of 2,819 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (1,089 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  2,799 km&amp;sup2; (1,081 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 20 km&amp;sup2; (8 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is water.  The total area is 0.72% water.
It is the oldest county in the State of Alabama. Over two thirds of the adult men had volunteered and served in the Confederate Army as of 1863, when the CSA was petitioned by a group of elderly men not to draft them, so that they might serve as a sort of homeland militia. They stated in their petition that they were needed to protect against potential slave uprisings, inasmuch as the county was home to numerous plantations with large slave populations.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]][[Geographic references#2|&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]] of [[2000]], there are 18,097 people, 6,705 households, and 5,042 families residing in the county.  The [[population density]] is 6/km&amp;sup2; (17/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 8,123 housing units at an average density of 3/km&amp;sup2; (8/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the county is 64.98% [[Race (U.S. census)|White]], 26.89% [[Race (U.S. census)|Black]] or [[Race (U.S. census)|African American]], 7.12% [[Race (U.S. census)|Native American]], 0.06% [[Race (U.S. census)|Asian]], 0.03% [[Race (U.S. census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.05% from [[race (U.S. census)|other races]], and 0.87% from two or more races.  0.88% of the population are [[Race (U.S. census)|Hispanic]] or [[Race (U.S. census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 6,705 households out of which 37.90% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 59.10% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 12.50% have a female householder with no husband present, and 24.80% are non-families. 22.80% of all households are made up of individuals and 10.10% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.69 and the average family size is 3.17.

In the county the population is spread out with 28.70% under the age of 18, 8.60% from 18 to 24, 27.40% from 25 to 44, 22.90% from 45 to 64, and 12.40% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 35 years.  For every 100 females there are 96.10 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 91.10 males.

The median income for a household in the county is $30,815, and the median income for a family is $37,881. Males have a median income of $35,237 versus $18,337 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the county is $14,081.  18.50% of the population and 14.80% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 21.50% of those under the age of 18 and 22.70% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

== Cities and towns ==
*[[Chatom, Alabama|Chatom]]
*[[McIntosh, Alabama|McIntosh]]
*[[Millry, Alabama|Millry]]

== See also ==
*[[Dry counties]]

{{Alabama}}

[[Category:Alabama counties]]

[[Category:Washington County, Alabama| ]]

[[de:Washington County (Alabama)]]</text>
