  <page>
    <title>Noyon</title>
    <id>81329</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36642072</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-25T14:04:21Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Olivier</username>
        <id>3808</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>commons</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Noyon''' is a small but historic [[France|French]] town in the [[Oise]] ''[[département in France|département]]'', [[Picardie]], on the [[Oise Canal]], approximately 60 miles north of [[Paris]].

==History==

Originally inhabited by a [[Gallic]] tribe, Noyon was so strongly fortified that in 531 bishop [[Medardus]] moved his see from the poorly-defended Vermand, in the [[Vermandois]], to Noyon. The cathedral at Noyon was the site where  Emperor [[Charlemagne]] was crowned in [[768]] as was the first [[Capetian]] king, [[Hugh Capet]] in [[987]].

Near the end of the [[16th century]] the city fell under the control of [[Spain]] but King [[Henry IV of France]] would recapture it. The city was occupied by the [[Germany|Germans]] during [[World War I]] and [[World War II]] and on both occasions suffered heavy damage.

== Famous personalities ==

*[[John Calvin]] was born in Noyon

{{commons|Category:Noyon|Noyon}}
[[Category:Communes of Oise]]

