      </contributor>
      <comment>/* External links */ if there's a separate article on the Innu language, the language categories shouldn't be here.</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Innu''' are the [[indigenous people|indigenous]] inhabitants of an area they refer to as ''[[Nitassinan]]'', which comprises most of the [[Quebec]]-[[Labrador]] peninsula in Eastern [[Canada]].  Their population in 2003 was between 15,000 and 20,000.  They are known to have lived on these lands as [[hunter-gatherers]] for several thousand years, living in tents made of animal skins.  Their most important resource is the [[caribou]], and the animal is culturally very significant to them.  Their language, ''[[Innu-aimun]]'', is spoken throughout Nitassinan, with certain dialect differences.

The Innu people are frequently incorrectly sub-divided into two groups, the ''Montagnais'' [&quot;mountain people&quot; in French] who live along the shore of the [[Gulf of Saint Lawrence]], and the less numerous ''Naskapi'' [&quot;inland people&quot; in Innu-aimun] who live farther north. Actually the Innu recognize several distinctions (e.g. Mushuau Innut, Maskuanu Innut, Uashau Innut), based on different regional affiliations and various dialects of the Innu language. Naskapi is probably not an indigenous term as it has been used by non-Innu observers, principally missionaries and traders, to refer to a host of different Innu regional groups. 

It seems to have first made an appearance in the 17th century and was subsequently applied to Innu groups beyond the reach of missionary influence. As this influence extended further and further inland the &quot;Naskapi&quot; ''retreated over the horizon'' until the only group left were those in the lands which bordered Ungava Bay and the more northerly parts of the Labrador coast. It is here that this term finally settled upon the northern most group of Innu, the Mushuau Innuts or People of the Tundra. The Mushuau Innuts include family groups who bridge the dialect change from y to n as in &quot;Iiyuu&quot; versus &quot;Innu&quot; and today there are Mushuauinnu families living in Kuauatshikamatsh, Natuashish and [[Sheshatshiu, Newfoundland and Labrador|Sheshatshiu]].

Some of the families in Kauauatshikamatsh are close relatives of Innu or Iyu who are now based in Kujjuarapik/Uapamekistiu, illustrating the point that even the division between James Bay Cree and Innu is an artificial one. The Innu dislike the practise of imposing European regional divisions, particularly ones based on recent jurisdictional claims made upon Innu territory by Europeans. Although confusion may arise due to the similarity of their names, the Innu do not have any common heritage with the [[Inuit]], a completely separate people whose coastal lands adjoin those of the Innu (though the Mushuauinnuts have a recent history of some intermarriage with Inuit). 

The Innu have never officially surrendered their territory to Canada. From the 1950s on, the [[Canadian government]], the provinces of Quebec and Newfoundland, and the [[Roman Catholic Church|Catholic]] and, in the north, the [[Anglican Church|Anglican]], churches attempted to &quot;civilise&quot; the Innu, inducing them to settle in fixed settlements and to abandon their [[nomad]]ic lifestyle. Before long, life in these artificially constructed settlements became marred by extremely high levels of [[alcoholism]], [[substance abuse]] by children, [[domestic violence]], and [[suicide]]. Between 1975 and 1995 the Innu settlements averaged 178 suicides per 100,000 persons per year.  This is more than twelve times the Canadian average. 

[[Survival International]] have alleged that the Canadian government's policy of relocating the Innu away from their ancestral lands and preventing them from practising their ancient way of life is in contravention of [[international law]], and they have drawn parallels with the treatment of the [[Tibetan people|Tibetans]] in the [[People's Republic of China]].

The best-known members of the Innu nation are the [[folk rock]] duo [[Kashtin]].

== External links ==
