  <page>
    <title>Orgel's rule</title>
    <id>81451</id>
    <revision>
      <id>31310766</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-14T09:59:29Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>82.25.246.104</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">== Orgel's First Rule ==
This states simply that, &quot;Whenever a [[spontaneous process]] is too slow or too inefficient a [[protein]] will evolve to speed it up or make it more efficient.&quot;

== Orgel's Second Rule ==
According to the [[evolutionary biology|evolutionary biologist]] [[Leslie Orgel]], &quot;[[Evolution]] is cleverer than you are&quot;.  This is known as '''Orgel's Second Rule''', and is intended as a rejoinder to the [[argument by lack of imagination]].

The same principle has been applied to [[software]] developed by evolution by group collaboration, as opposed to software built to a pre-ordained design that was created without reference to previous implementation.

==External link==
* [http://www.salk.edu/faculty/orgel.html Leslie Orgel's homepage]
[[Category:Evolution]]

[[de:Orgels Regel]]</text>
    </revision>
