  <page>
    <title>Acetyl</title>
    <id>81632</id>
    <revision>
      <id>34509383</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-09T17:15:27Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>218.103.137.150</ip>
      </contributor>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Acetyl''' ([[IUPAC]] name '''ethanoyl''') is the [[acyl]] of [[acetic acid]], with [[chemical formula]] -[[Carbon|C]][[Oxygen|O]][[Carbon|C]][[Hydrogen|H]]&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt;, sometimes abbreviated to '''Ac''' (not to be confused with the [[Chemical element|element]] [[actinium]]). A molecule containing an acetyl group has the [[chemical structure]]

:[[Image:Acetyl.png]]. 

(where R denotes the remainder of the molecule.) 

The acetyl radical contains a [[methyl]] group single-bonded to a [[carbonyl]]. The [[carbon]] of the carbonyl has an lone [[electron]] available, with which it forms a [[chemical bond]] to the remainder of the [[molecule]].

The acetyl radical is a component of many [[organic compound]]s, including [[Acetyl_coenzyme_A|acetyl-CoA]], the [[neurotransmitter]] [[acetylcholine]], and the [[analgesic|analgesics]] [[acetaminophen]] and acetylsalicylic acid (better known as [[aspirin]]).

The introduction of an acetyl group into a molecule is called [[acetylation]] (or IUPAC name ethanoylation).


[[category:functional groups]]

[[da:Acetyl]]
[[nl:Acetyl]]</text>
    </revision>
