      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Ancient Greece]] and/or [[Greek mythology]], the name '''Lycurgus'''/'''Lykurgus''' can refer to:

#An alternate name for [[Lycomedes]].
#[[Lycurgus (Thrace)]]: an Edonian King who banned the cult of [[Dionysus]], and paid dearly.
#[[Lycurgus (Sparta)]]: the legendary lawgiver.
#[[Lycurgus of Athens]]: an [[Athens|Athenian]] statesman and one of the ten [[Attic orators]] (c.[[390 BC|390]] - c.[[325 BC]]); ''Against Leocrates'' is the only surviving speech by this Lycurgus, of the fifteen that [[Caecilius of Calacte]] considered genuine.
#[[Lycurgus (King in Arcadia)]]
#[[Lycurgus (son of Pronax)]]
#[[Lycurgus (King of Nemea)]]
#Lycurgus, [[New York]]: a fictional town in the [[Theodore Dreiser]]'s novel, [[An American Tragedy]]

{{hndis}}

