      <comment>robot  Adding: fr</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{Greek myth (sea)}}
:''This article is about the mythological figures. For the moon of Neptune, see [[Psamathe (moon)]]''.
In [[Greek mythology]], there were two people named '''Psamathe'''.

#Psamathe was a [[Nereid]], the lover of [[Aeacus]] and mother of [[Phocus]] ([[Ovid]] XI, 398).  In the tragedy ''[[Helen (play)|Helen]]'' by [[Euripides]], she was married to king [[Proteus of Egypt]].  
#Daughter of [[Crotopus]], the King of [[Argos]] and mother of [[Linus]] by [[Apollo (god)|Apollo]].  She feared her father and gave the infant Linus to shepherds to raise. He was torn apart by dogs after reaching adulthood and Psamathe was killed by her father, for which Apollo sent a child-killing plague to Argos.

Some translations of Ovid have the name as '''Psamanthe'''.  This is seen in Ovid XI. [http://etext.lib.virginia.edu/latin/ovid/sandys/11comm.htm]

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]
[[Category:Nymphs]]

[[fr:Psamathée]]
[[it:Psamate]]
[[lt:Psamatė]]</text>
    </revision>
