  <page>
    <title>Solipsism</title>
    <id>81798</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42095451</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-03T20:18:00Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Normxxx</username>
        <id>656369</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Eastern philosophies */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Solipsism''' (from the [[Latin]] ''ipse'' = &quot;self&quot; and ''solus'' = &quot;alone&quot;) is an extreme form of [[skepticism]], saying that nothing exists beyond oneself and one's immediate experiences.  More generally, it is the [[epistemology|epistemological]] belief that one's self is the only thing that can be known with certainty and verified (sometimes called ''egoism'').  Solipsism is also commonly understood to encompass the [[metaphysics|metaphysical]] belief that only one's self exists, and that &quot;existence&quot; just means being a part of one's own mental states &amp;mdash; all objects, people, etc, that one experiences are merely parts of one's own mind. Solipsism is first recorded with the presocratic [[sophist]] [[Gorgias]] (c. [[483 BC|483]]-[[375 BC]]) who is quoted by [[Sextus Empiricus]] as having stated:

# Nothing exists
# Even if something exists, nothing can be known about it, and
# Even if something could be known about it, knowledge about it can't be communicated to others

Solipsism is generally identified with statements 2 and 3 from Gorgias.

But, in introducing methodological doubt (via ''[[Cogito ergo sum]]'') into philosophy, [[Descartes]] created the backdrop against which modern interpretations of solipsism subsequently developed and was made to seem, if not plausible, at least irrefutable.  (Descartes' God is completely dispensible.)

Solipsism is [[logical]]ly [[Coherence (linguistics)|coherent]], but not [[falsifiable]], so it is not [[testable]] by current modes of the [[scientific method]].

== The Solipsist versus 'The Last Man Alive' ==

Is the last man alive a solipsist (e.g., say, after a nuclear holocaust)? ''Not necessarily,'' because for the solipsist, it is not merely the case that he believes that his thoughts, experiences, and emotions are, as a matter of contingent fact, the only thoughts, experiences, and emotions that can be. Rather, the solipsist can attach ''no meaning'' to the supposition that there could be thoughts, experiences, and emotions ''other than'' his own— that events may occur or objects or people exist independently of ''his'' 'imagination.' In short, the true solipsist understands the word 'pain', for example, to mean '''my'' pain'— he cannot accordingly conceive how this word is to be applied in any sense other than this exclusively egocentric, non-empathetic one.

Assuming the validity of solipsism, one must infer that it makes as much or as little sense, on these premises, to attribute any psychological predicate to another human being as it does to attribute it to a table or a rock. Which is to say that, on these premises, ''it makes no sense to attribute consciousness to another human being at all.'' Which would ''not'' be the response of a non-solipsistic 'last man alive,' if such a one suddenly stumbled across another human being (or simply recalled one from memory).[http://www.iep.utm.edu/s/solipsis.htm]

== Importance of the Problem ==
The foundations of solipsism lie at the heart of the view that the individual understands all psychological concepts (thinking, willing, perceiving, etc.) by analogy with his own mental states, i.e. by abstraction from ''inner experience''. And this view, or some variant of it, has been held by a great many, if not indeed the majority of, philosophers, since Descartes elevated the egocentric search for incontrovertible certainty to the status of the ''primary goal'' of critical epistemology. In this sense, then, it is at least contestable that solipsism is implicit in many philosophies of knowledge and mind since Descartes, and that any philosophy which adopts the Cartesian egocentric approach as its basic frame of reference is inherently solipsistic.

The problem of solipsism also merits close examination because it is based upon three widely held philosophical '''''pre'''''suppositions, which are themselves fundamental and wide-ranging in importance. These are: (1) That what ''I'' know ''most certainly'' are the contents of my ''own'' mind— ''my'' thoughts, experiences, affects, etc.; (2) That there is no conceptual or logically necessary link between the mental and the physical— between, say, the occurrence of certain conscious experiences or mental states and the 'possession' and behavioral dispositions of a 'body' of a particular kind (see the [[#Brain in a vat|''thought experiment'']] which follows); and (3) That the experiences of a given person are ''necessarily'' private to that person. These presuppositions are unmistakably ''Cartesian'' in origin, and are very widely accepted by philosophers and non-philosophers alike.[http://www.iep.utm.edu/s/solipsis.htm#H1]

==Brain in a vat==
A [[thought-experiment]] related to solipsism, although in principle distinct (for one thing, it posits a ''real'' mad scientist, brain, and vat), is the ''[[brain in a vat]]''.  The person performing the thought-experiment considers the possibility that they are trapped within some utterly unknowable reality, much like that illustrated in the movie ''[[The Matrix]]''.  A mad scientist could be sending the same impulses to one's brain in a vat that one's brain (understood to be that of a person in the &quot;real world&quot;) might receive, thereby ''creating'' &quot;the world&quot; as one knows it from the mad scientist's program.  Yet, for one's brain in the vat, that &quot;world&quot; would obviously not be &quot;real.&quot;  This raises the possibility that everything one thinks or knows is [[illusion]].  Or, at the least, that one cannot know with any certainty whether one's brain is in the &quot;real world&quot; or in a vat receiving impulses that would create an equivalent consciousness— or even if there is a ''real'' world, mad scientist, brain, or vat (all experience could be simply a never-ending dream).

== Solipsism versus other philosophies ==
===Cartesian Dualism===
[[Dualism (philosophy of mind)|Dualists]] commonly argue for the distinction of [[mind]] (or '[[idea]]s') and matter by employing [[Leibniz]]'s [[Identity of indiscernibles]] according to which two things are identical if, and only if, they simultaneously share exactly the same qualities (i.e., are indistinguishable).   Dualists then attempt to identify attributes of mind that are lacked by matter (such as privacy or intentionality) or vice versa (such as having a certain temperature or electrical charge).[http://www.newdualism.org/papers/J.DePoe/dualism.htm] [http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/05169a.htm] But one famous application of the identity of indiscernibles was by [[René Descartes]] in his ''[[Meditations on First Philosophy]]''.  Descartes concluded that he could not doubt the existence of ''himself'' (the famous ''[[cogito ergo sum]]'' argument), but that he ''could'' doubt the (separate) existence of ''his'' body.  From this he inferred that the ''person Descartes'' must not be identical to ''the  Descartes body'', since one possessed a characteristic that the other did not: namely, it could be known to exist. But such an argument leads to solipsism, not dualism, since then ''the  Descartes body'' could only exist as an idea in the mind of the ''person Descartes''[http://www.iep.utm.edu/d/dualism.htm][http://www.iep.utm.edu/s/solipsis.htm].
 
===Idealism===
In the philosophical system developed by Irish philosopher [[George Berkeley]], [[Idealism]], Berkeley states that physical objects, matter, do not exist independent of the mind. (Both Descartes and Berkeley fall prey to the [[egocentric predicament]].)  Both (but especially Berkeley) attempt to show things can and do exist apart from the human mind and our perception, but only because there is an all-encompassing Mind in which all 'ideas' are perceived — or a deity that creates perception in the human mind — either way it's God.[http://www.answers.com/topic/george-berkeley][http://www.philosophos.com/philosophy_article_27.html]

=== Materialism versus Idealism===
Most philosophers, for the last two millennia at least, have been— and still are— quarreling about whether the world is actually there, the ''material machine'' we experience during working hours at least, or if it is a bunch of ''ideas'', something not material anyway, the material world being some kind of perceptive illusion (like a dream we do not awaken from). The two warring factions are called [[materialism]][http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/10041b.htm] and [[idealism]][http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07634a.htm]; none too original. Idealism often— after its most famous proponent— is called [[Platonism]][http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/12159a.htm]. Religious thinking tends to be some form of idealism (e.g., [[Neoplatonism]]).[http://www.sullivan-county.com/id3/neoplatonism.htm] [http://www.iep.utm.edu/g/germidea.htm] [http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/10041b.htm]
 
=== Dualism versus Monism===
In general, dualists define the universe as being some constitution of ego and not-ego. (In this regard, even [[Pluralism (philosophy of mind)|pluralism]] may be said to be captured by this paradigm.[http://everything2.com/index.pl?node_id=1778047][http://www.kirjasto.sci.fi/berlin.htm][http://www4.hmc.edu:8001/humanities/beckman/PhilNotes/russell.htm])  (See, e.g., [[Plato]]'s ''[[Phaedo]],'' Descartes' ''Meditations on First Philosophy,'' [[John Locke|Locke]]'s ''[[Essay Concerning Human Understanding]],'' [[Hume]]'s ''[[A Treatise of Human Nature]]'' and, more recently, [[Karl Popper]]'s and [[Eccles|W.J. Eccles]]' (1977): ''The Self and its Brain'') But, we have only to substitute the solipsist's 'unconscious' for the dualist's non-ego as the author of all (apparently) external events and objects to see that, by  Leibniz's Law, there is nothing to distinguish the dualist from the solipsist. Since the 'mind', construed along Cartesian lines, leads to solipsism[http://www.iep.utm.edu/s/solipsis.htm], it is sometimes thought better to operationalize the mind and define mental states behaviorally, functionally, or physiologically (e.g., via [http://www.newdualism.org/papers/T.Rauchenstein/mbd.htm Physicalism or Epiphenomenalism]) If mental states are just behavioral states, brain states, or functional states, then we can verify that others have mental states on the basis of publicly observable phenomena, thereby avoiding skepticism about other selves.  But this merely reduces to a materialistic monism ([[Pierre Jean George Cabanis|Cabanis]], for instance, proclaims [[Cartesian materialism|his materialism]] in the well-known Crude formula: &quot;The brain digests impressions, and organically secretes thought.&quot;), which is effectively ''[[congruent]]'' to solipsism (see below).

===Ancient roots of Idealism and Materialism===
For millennia, thinkers have been arguing whether there is or is not more to the world than proverbially &quot;meets the eye.&quot; Plato, the classic champion of idealism, argues in Book V of [[the Republic]] that those who believe only in the sensory nature of the universe are literally &quot;living in a dream.&quot; Likewise, the [[Hindu]] worldview is based on the notion that all matter (and thus humans) are structures of the universe, thus we are all subtly interconnected not only to the world around us, but to all other matter in the universe; that all we can perceive is a kind of vision, [[Samsara]]. This idealistic conception of the universe usually also involves an imperceptible realm, in Plato's case the Forms, that we will never be able to directly apprehend, but whose ultimate reality makes our perceptions all the more real.  

===Solipsism, WYSIWYG, and Phenomenalism===
Solipsism goes but one step further in declaring that there is ''no'' absolute ego, ''no'' absolute non-ego (i.e., no &quot;imperceptable realm,&quot; no &quot;ultimate reality&quot;). There is no more than the individual consciousness (cf. von Schubert Soldern).[http://www.history-of-philosophy.com/phenomenalism.htm] (WYSIWYG, or, What You See Is What You Get.[http://wysiwyg.stanford.edu/]) Admitting the principles, an escape from such a conclusion is difficult. The 'reine Erfahrung' of [[Avenarius]], the 'pure experience' for you and for me, is theoretic and inevident. Indeed [[Humanism]] itself, as advanced by [[Schiller]], seems to be but a kind of solipsism. The data of thought are [[immanent]], and we only organized them; but Schiller gives no indication of their origin; indeed he says it is absurd to ask whence the given of thought derives. The whole modern school of Immanence (q.v.) belongs to the development of this form of [[Phenomenalism]].[http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/11791b.htm]

=== Materialism ===
[[Democritus]]' student, [[Lucretius]] [http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/lucretius/], asserted that matter exists in the form of [[atomism|atoms]], which move around the universe in an empty space, and that [[On the Nature of Things|all things]] (including the mind) are made up of atoms. Materialism, which arises from such beginnings, seems straightforward, but then most people find they have the [[Mind-body problem|mind-body]], &quot;[[Ghost in the machine]],&quot; [[The Concept of Mind|paradox]] as a problem.[http://www.philosophy.unimelb.edu.au/tgelder/papers/MDP.html][http://www.artsci.wustl.edu/~philos/MindDict/materialism.html] They shouldn't, because ''that'' specific problem arises from ''taking for granted'' that minds somehow are ''non-material'' ''objects'' in the ''same [[The Concept of Mind|category]]'' as the material body (that is: idealistic ghosts in the materialistic cupboard, outright magical, non-physical ghosts)[http://website.lineone.net/~kwelos/materialism.htm]. If you stay a sane, 'naive' materialist[http://www.kheper.net/topics/worldviews/materialism.htm], realizing that the mind is a ''material machine in action''— the brain PC writing to its ''own'' screen— no dualism is perceived. (WYSIWYG, or, What You See Is What You Get.) To the 'naive' materialist, the 'mind' is no more ephemeral a thing than is the execution of a computer program. (But see also [http://members.aol.com/NeoNoetics/MindsBrainsPrograms.html] and [http://www.ditext.com/popper/lbp.html]) These are actually much more [[Ordinary language philosophy|sophisticated argument]]s than, say, ones from [[neutral monism]].

===Solipsism, WYSIWYG, and 'naive' Materialism===
Solipsism is idealism taken to extremes[http://atheism.about.com/library/glossary/general/bldef_solipsism.htm], only '''one''' dreamy substance existing, that of ones ''own'' ego and ''its'' entire universe. In so simplifying things, it becomes ''fully congruous'' with a straight-forward, ''non-dualistic'' materialism, 'naive materialism,' as cocky sophisticates sometimes like to call it[http://www.aboutadidam.org/lesser_alternatives/scientific_materialism/simple_materialism.html]. That is the beauty of it. It suddenly emerges as a ''perfect unity,'' a ''fusion'' of two ways of incompatible thinking, as there is no compartmentalization between the ego and the rest of the universe.[http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/10483a.htm] (WYSIWYG, or, What You See Is What You Get.) As has been remarked, &quot;It seems that there are two ways into solipsism. One is to reduce everything to mind, and the other is to reduce everything to matter.&quot;([http://www.johnrowan.org.uk/background.html Rowan], 1994)[http://www.gwiep.net/site/idearout.htm] 

Which is to say, that the choice of 'worldview' is probably arbitrary: everyone will still behave the same in the end. All 'worldviews' may be shown to be roughly congruent, at least as they affect the everyday behavior of their adherents! And all seem to nicely account for all of the everyday phenomena recognized, resulting in paradoxes only if their results are reexamined using another 'worldview.' ''Even the prudent solipsist looks both ways before crossing the street.''

==Eastern philosophies==
Thoughts similar to solipsism are present in much of [[eastern philosophy]].  [[Taoism]] and several interpretations of [[Buddhism]], especially [[Zen]], teach that drawing a distinction between [[self]] and [[universe]] is [[nonsense|nonsensical]] and arbitrary, and merely an artifact of language rather than an inherent reality.

&quot;He who sees everything as nothing but the Self, and the Self in everything he sees, such a seer withdraws from nothing. 

&quot;''For the enlightened, all that exists is nothing but the Self,'' so how could any suffering or delusion continue for those who know this oneness?&quot; 

''Isha Upanishad, sloka 6''

== Objections ==
The following arguments and counters to solipsism are the highlights of anti-solipsist thought:

;Only of a ''living human being'' and what ''resembles (behaves like)'' a living human being can one say: it has sensations; it sees; is blind; hears; is deaf; is conscious or unconscious. (Wittgenstein, § 281): The intersubjective world in which we live with other human beings, the public language-system which we must master if we are to think at all, these are the primary data, the ''proto-phenomena'', in Wittgenstein's phrase (§ 654). Our psychological and non-psychological concepts alike are derived from a single linguistic source.  The solipsist would only marvel at the ability of his unconscious to script such a rich set of experiences.

;People die :However, the objector has not died, and therefore has not disproved it. The solipsist ''cannot'' 'die', because when ''he'' 'dies', the entire universe ceases to exist.  Therefore, death is another illusion of the solipsist's unconscious.  Since the being who has 'died' is only a phantom of the solipsist's imagination anyway, the elimination of that phantom proves nothing.

;Life causes [[pain]] :Why would a solipsist create pain for himself? One response to this is that there may be some reason which he has decided to forget on purpose, such as the (unconsciously created) law of [[Karma]], or a desire not to be bored. Another response is that the category of 'pain' is a perception assumed with all of the other socio-cultural human values the solipsist has created for himself.[http://www.philosophos.com/philosophy_article_27.html] A solipsistic value system may or may not recognize pain, or the alleged fact of personal death, as 'true.'

;The practical solipsist needs a [[language]] to formulate his thoughts about solipsism :Language is an essential tool to communicate with other minds. Why does a solipsist universe need a language? Indeed, one might even say, solipsism is ''necessarily incoherent'', for to make an appeal to logical rules or empirical evidence the solipsist would implicitly have to affirm the very thing in which he purportedly refuses to believe: the 'reality' of intersubjectively valid criteria, and/or of a public, extra-mental world.[http://www.phil.uni-passau.de/dlwg/ws01/12-1-94.txt][http://www.phil.uni-passau.de/dlwg/ws01/12-1-94.txt] Possible responses are similar to the last objection; that is, to keep from becoming bored, perhaps the solipsist imagines &quot;other&quot; minds, which would actually be only elements of his own mind, and which he has chosen to forget control of for the time being, inventing an elaborate language in order to interact with these more isolated segments of his mind, or even to ''dialog'' with his conscious self (i.e., talk to himself). A more telling question might be, why does the solipsist need to invent so many and such a variety of languages?

;Realism vs. solipsism :An objection, raised by [[David Deutsch]], among others, is that since the solipsist has no control over the &quot;universe&quot; he is creating for himself, there must be some [[unconscious mind|unconscious]] part of his mind creating it. If the solipsist makes his unconscious mind the object of scientific study (e.g., by conducting experiments), he will find that it behaves with the same complexity as the universe offered by [[realism]]; therefore, the distinction between realism and solipsism collapses. What realism calls &quot;the universe&quot;, solipsism calls &quot;one's unconscious mind.&quot; But these are just different names for the same thing. Both are massively complex processes other than the solipsist's conscious mind, and the cause of all the solipsist's experiences; possibly merely a labelling distinction.  The solipsist would claim that &quot;only&quot; oneself exists and the apparent independence of ''real'' world events is just an illusion.  The real world may be every bit as complex as my unconscious, but when '''''I''''' die (if there is meaning in that phrase), the entire universe ceases to exist. (See also, Le Guin, Ursula K. ''Lathe of Heaven'' Eos; Reprint edition (April 1, 1997))

;Philosophical analysis: Some philisophers hold the viewpoint that  solipsism is entirely empty and without content.  Like a 'faith' argument, it seems ''sterile,'' i.e., allows  no further argument nor can it be falsified.[http://fcrp.quaker.org/InwardLight049/049Kotschnig.html][http://www.iep.utm.edu/e/eth-phen.htm] The world remains absolutely the same&amp;mdash; so where could a solipsist go from there? Viewed in this way, solipsism seems only to have found a facile way to avoid the more difficult task of a [[Analytic philosophy|critical analysis]] of what is 'real' and what isn't, and what '[[reality]]' means.  The solipsist would hold that further argument is meaningless and there are limits to what can be known about 'reality.'

== References ==
#Carus, Titus Lucretius, aka 'Lucretius' (~50 B.C.E) ''On the Nature of Things'' [http://classics.mit.edu/Carus/nature_things.html]
#Popper, K. R. &amp; Eccles, J. C. (1977) ''The self and its brain.'' Heidelberg: Springer-Verlag. UCE, GM]
#Bertrand, Arthur Russel. ''The Analysis of Mind.'' Rutledge, London, New York, 1995
#Bertrand, Arthur Russel. ''Problems of Philosophy.'' Hackett Classics, Indianapolis, 1990
#von Schubert Soldern, Richard ''Ueber Transcendenz des Objects und Subjects'' (Leipzig, 1882)
#Wittgenstein, L. ''Philosophical Investigations.'' Blackwell, 1974.

