  <page>
    <title>Aksel Schiøtz</title>
    <id>81802</id>
    <revision>
      <id>37605623</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-01T00:57:31Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Valentinian</username>
        <id>256198</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>stub sorting</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Aksel Schiøtz''' ([[1906]]&amp;ndash;[[1975]]) was a [[Denmark|Danish]] [[opera]]tic [[tenor]].

Schiøtz was born in [[Roskilde]], [[Denmark]]. Having obtained an M.A. in Danish and English in 1930, he made his début at the [[Det Kongelige Theater]], [[Copenhagen]], in [[Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart|Mozart]]'s ''[[Cosi fan tutte]]'' in [[1931]]. At the [[Glyndebourne]] Festival in [[1946]], he alternated with [[Peter Pears]] in the part of 'Male Chorus' in [[Benjamin Britten]]'s ''[[The Rape of Lucretia]].''

More than as an opera singer, however, Schiøtz is remembered for his interpretaion of Danish songs and [[Franz Schubert|Schubert]]'s and [[Robert Schumann|Schumann]]'s ''[[Lied]]er,'' as well as songs by [[Carl Michael Bellman]]. During the [[Nazi]] occupation of Denmark ([[1940]]&amp;ndash;[[1945]]), he achieved great popularity for his recording of traditional Danish songs of the 19th and early 20th centuries.

From [[1955]] to [[1957]] he served as professor of music at the [[University of Minnesota]], Minneapolis. Schiøtz died in Copenhagen.


{{opera-singer-stub}}
{{Denmark-bio-stub}}
