  <page>
    <title>Freedom newspaper</title>
    <id>81846</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36535379</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-24T19:42:15Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>RJII</username>
        <id>141644</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Freedom_newspaper_13th_september_2003.jpg|right|thumb|150px|Cover of ''Freedom'', dated 13th September 2003, showing the new cover design by [[Clifford Harper]].]] 
'''''Freedom''''' (ISSN 0016 0504) is a [[London]]-based [[anarchism|anarchist]] [[newspaper]] published every two weeks by [[Freedom Press]]. 

The paper started in [[1886]] by volunteers including [[Peter Kropotkin]] and [[Charlotte Wilson]] and continues to this day as an unpaid project. Originally, the subtitle was &quot;A Journal of [[libertarian socialism|Anarchist Socialism]].&quot; The title was changed to &quot;A Journal of [[anarcho-communism|Anarchist Communism]]&quot; in June 1889. Today it is labeled simply as &quot;anarchist news and views&quot;. 

The newspaper's [[mission statement]] is stated in every issue, on page 2, and summarises the writers' view of anarchism:

:''Anarchists work towards a society of [[mutual aid]] and voluntary co-operation. We reject all government and economic repression. This newspaper, published continuously since 1936, exists to explain anarchism more widely and show that only in an anarchist society can human freedom thrive.''

The paper features news from [[peace movement|peace]] and [[labour movement]]s and events, as well as listing planned events and [[protest]]s on the back page. Staying true to Kropotkin's principle of [[mutual aid]], the paper regularly features reviews of other anarchist and [[libertarianism|libertarian]] publications, such as ''Organise!'' and ''[[Direct Action (periodical)|Direct Action]]'' as well as other local and international newsletters and journals.

[[Image:Freedom vol63 no21.gif|left|thumb|150px|Cover of ''Freedom'' dated 16th November 2002 (old design)]]

It regularly features [[Donald Rooum]]'s [[Wildcat]] [[cartoon strip]] as well as Rooum's articles, including recent ones on [[genetic modification|genetically modified]] foods. 

Along with a number of gradual changes in the content and structure of the paper and organisational changes at Freedom Press, ''Freedom'' got a re-design in September 2003, which can be seen to the right.

==See also==
*''[[The Raven (journal)|The Raven]]''

==External links==
*[http://www.freedompress.org.uk freedompress.org.uk - the Freedom Press website]
*[http://groups.yahoo.com/group/FreedomAnarchistFortnightly Yahoo! group, where articles are discussed]
*[http://dwardmac.pitzer.edu/Anarchist_Archives/journals/freedom/index.html Old issues of Freedom], preserved at the Anarchist Archives. Includes the first ever issue.
*[http://www.libcom.org libcom.org - Online home of anarchism in Britain, formerly enrager.net]

[[Category:Anarchist publications]]
[[Category:British newspapers]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>The Raven (journal)</title>
    <id>81849</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39059155</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-10T12:17:30Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Liftarn</username>
        <id>5861</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>Wikilinking</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[image:foodraven1.jpg|thumb|200px|Cover of the final issue of ''The Raven'', issue 43, 'Food']]
'''''The Raven''''' was a quarterly [[anarchist]] review published by [[Freedom Press]]. Each edition usually focused on a specific issue such as [[food]], [[health]], [[economics]], [[genetic engineering]], [[psychology]], land and so on, from an anarchist viewpoint.

After a lull in the publishing of the review, in September 2003, Freedom Press announced that it would no longer be publishing ''The Raven''. The Press cited both a review of costs (in a letter to ''Raven'' subscribers) and changes in the direction and content of the [[Freedom newspaper|''Freedom'' newspaper]]
:''Our intention is that changes in this newspaper will allow it to do some of the more reflective work that was previously The Raven's lot.'' (''Freedom'' 64(17), 13th September 2003.)

{{mag-stub}}

[[Category:Anarchist publications]]</text>
    </revision>
