    <title>Thomas Savery</title>
    <id>81860</id>
    <revision>
      <id>36148170</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-22T00:03:13Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Mais oui!</username>
        <id>394460</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>cats, links</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Thomas Savery''' (c.[[1650]]-[[1715]]) was an [[England|English]] [[inventor]], born in [[Shilston]], [[Devon]], [[England]].

Initially interested in naval applications of engineering (he designed an early [[paddle-wheel]]), Savery then became interested in [[pump]]ing machines. On [[2 July]] [[1698]] he patented an early [[steam engine]] for pumping water out of [[mine]]s (hence their occasional name, the 'miner's friend'). Later he developed an improved version in partnership with [[Thomas Newcomen]].

Savery obtained some of his ideas from the work of [[Denis Papin]], a [[France|French]] [[physicist]] who invented a type of [[pressure cooking|pressure cooker]] in [[1679]] and a model [[piston engine]].

[[Category:1650 births|Savery, Thomas]]
[[Category:1715 deaths|Savery, Thomas]]
[[Category:English engineers|Savery, Thomas]]
[[Category:English inventors|Savery, Thomas]]
[[Category:Natives of Devon|Savery, Thomas]]

