      <comment>ru:</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">In [[Greek mythology]], '''Tydeus''' was the father of [[Diomedes]] and husband of [[Deipyle]].  He was a son of [[Oeneus]] and [[Periboea]].  He was one of the [[Seven Against Thebes]]; during the battle to take the city, he was killed by [[Melanippus]].

In the ''[[Iliad]]'', Homer makes such frequent and detailed references to the exploits of Tydeus (in connection with his son) that some commentators have speculated that Homer must have composed another work having Tydeus as its main subject.


