  <page>
    <title>Phaedra (mythology)</title>
    <id>81935</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41238255</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-26T00:08:07Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>The Singing Badger</username>
        <id>57730</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* The Phaedra story in literature */  shorten title</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">:''This article is on the mythological figure. For other meanings, see [[Phaedra]].''

In [[Greek mythology]], '''Phaedra''' was the mother of [[Demophon]] and [[Acamas]] by [[Theseus]].

While married to Theseus, Phaedra fell in love with [[Hippolytus (mythology)|Hippolytus]], Theseus' son by his ex-wife, [[Hippolyte]]. According to some sources, Hippolytus had scorned [[Aphrodite]] to become a devotee of [[Artemis]] and Aphrodite made Phaedra fall in love with him as a punishment.  He rejected her.  Alternatively, Phaedra's nurse told Hippolytus of her love, and he swore he would not reveal her as a source of information&amp;mdash;even after Phaedra killed herself and blamed his seduction of her in her suicide note.  In revenge, Phaedra wrote Theseus a letter that claimed Hippolytus raped her.  She then killed herself.  Theseus believed her and cursed Hippolytus with one of the three curses he had received from [[Poseidon]]. As a result, Hippolytus' horses were frightened by a sea monster and dragged their rider to his death.  Alternatively, after Phaedra told Theseus that Hippolytus had raped her, Theseus killed his son and Phaedra committed suicide out of guilt for she had not intended for Hippolytus to die.  [[Artemis]] later told Theseus the truth.  In a third version, Phaedra simply told Theseus this and did not kill herself; [[Dionysus]] sent a wild bull which terrified Hippolytus' horses.

==Phaedra in literature==

Phaedra's story appears in several major works of literature, including:

* [[Euripides]], ''[[Hippolytus (play)|Hippolytus]]'', a [[Greek language|Greek]] play
* [[Seneca the Younger]], ''[[Phaedra (Seneca)|Phaedra]]'', a [[Latin]] play
* [[Jean Racine]], ''[[Phèdre]]'' ([[1677]]), a [[French language|French]] play
* [[Per Olov Enquist]], ''[[Till Fedra]]'' ([[1980]]), a [[Swedish language|Swedish]] play
* [[Sarah Kane]], ''[[Phaedra's Love]]'' ([[1996]]), an [[English language|English]] play

== Phaedra in music ==

Phaedra is also the subject of a number of musical works, including:

* ''Phaedra'', [[Benjamin Britten]], 1976
* ''Phaedra'', [[Tangerine Dream]], 1974
* &quot;Some Velvet Morning&quot;, [[Nancy Sinatra]] and [[Lee Hazlewood]]

==References==
* [[Virgil]], Aeneid VII.765; [[Ovid]], Metamorphoses XV.497

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]

