  <page>
    <title>Acamas</title>
    <id>81937</id>
    <revision>
      <id>42157492</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-04T05:00:08Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>203.115.162.67</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Acamas (son of Antenor) */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">There are several characters named Acamas (&quot;unwearying&quot;) in [[Greek mythology]].  The following three all fought in the [[Trojan War]], and only the first is not mentioned by [[Homer]].

==Acamas (son of Theseus)==

'''Acamas''' was the son of [[Phaedra]] and [[Theseus]].  When his father was exiled from [[Athens]], he was sent to [[Euboea]] with his half-brother [[Demophoon]].  There, they grew to adulthood and joined Euboea's King [[Elephenor]] in the [[Trojan War]], where they fought on the side of the Greeks.  Acamas and Demophoon are not mentioned in Homer's ''[[Iliad]]'', but later authors mention that Acamas was one of the men inside the [[Trojan Horse]].  Acamas was either betrothed or married to [[Phyllis]], but he abandoned her.


==Acamas (son of Eusorus)==

This '''Acamas''' was son of [[Eusorus]], and resident of [[Aenus (Thrace)|Aenus]] on the eastern coast of [[Thrace]].  With his comrade [[Peiros]], son of Imbrasus, Acamas led a contingent of Thracian warriors to the Trojan War.  Acamas was killed by [[Odysseus]].

==Acamas (son of Antenor)==

With his brother [[Archelochus]] and his cousin [[Aeneas]], '''Acamas''', son of [[Antenor]], led the [[Dardanian]] contingent to assist King [[Priam]].  He was killed by [[Odysseus]].


{{Greek-myth-stub}}
[[Category:People who fought in the Trojan War]]

[[es:Acamante]]
[[gl:Acamante]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
