  <page>
    <title>Biopiracy</title>
    <id>82104</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41893041</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T12:24:16Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <ip>130.125.1.42</ip>
      </contributor>
      <comment>/* Historical background  / CBD (1992) */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{NPOV}}

'''Biopiracy''' refers to the privatization and unauthorized use of [[biological resource]]s by  entities (including [[corporation]]s, [[university|universities]] and [[government]]s) outside of a country which has pre-existing knowledge. This privatization and use is sometimes claimed to be predatory. Particular activities usually covered by the term are:
* Exclusive commercial rights to [[plant]]s, [[animal]]s, [[organ (anatomy)|organ]]s, [[microorganism]]s, and [[gene]]s;
* [[Commercialization]] of [[traditional knowledge|traditional communities' knowledge]] on biological resources;
* [[Patent]]ing of biological resources.

== A possible biopiracy scenario ==

An agricultural biotechnology company learns of a [[plant]] with unique traits from the [[indigenous people|indigenous]] community of the [[biodiversity]]-rich country. After discovering the plant's traits have commercial application in [[developed nations|developed countries]], the company acquires samples of it and returns to their country. 

The company isolates and purifies the [[gene]] encoding the desirable characteristics and applies for a [[biological patent]].  The company introduces patented and [[trademark]]ed products incorporating the gene into the market, and the company [[profit]]s. None of the money is returned to the original indigenous peoples because the company had no legal obligations&amp;mdash;[[contract]]ual or otherwise.

Real scenarios are in the external links section.

=== Results ===
As a result of biopiracy, and absent agreements and laws to the contrary, the source country and peoples receive no further financial benefit and may even be obliged to pay for the use of the same products because of enforcement of [[TRIPs]] and [[GATT]]. The company, on the other hand, has the [[right]] to exclude others from making, using or selling products incorporating the isolated and purified gene which is again enforced by [[TRIPs]] and [[GATT]]. 

However, not everyone in a developing nation is indigenous (for example, [w:India]), and as such the term indigenous is often used as a subjective term and has variant definitions around the world.

== Historical background  / CBD (1992) ==

In the 1980s, an erosion of biodiversity was observed.  The majority of the actors estimated that the first cause of biodiversity erosion was the lack or the wrong definition of the rights of ownership.  Indeed, before 1992, the living resources were regarded as ''Common Heritage of Mankind''.  As common resources, private companies could take and use each resource without having any justification or compensation to give.

The [[Convention on Biological Diversity]] (CBD) entered into force in [[1993]]. It gave [[sovereign national rights]] over biological resources. One of the advantages of it was that it would enable southern countries to better benefit from their resources and traditional knowledge.

Under these new rules, it is expected  that bioprospecting implies a prior [[informed consent]], and must result in a share of the benefits between the biodiversity-rich country and the prospecting firm. However, some critics believe that the CBD has failed to establish appropriate regulations to prevent biopiracy.

== Biopiracy in the news ==

India has been translating ancient manuscripts containing old remedies over to computers, partially to prevent biopiracy. The texts are being recorded from Sanskrit, Urdu, Persian and Arabic; they will be made available to patent offices in English, German, French, Japanese and Spanish in 2006. The aim is to protect India's heritage from being exploited by foreign companies. Hundreds of Yoga poses are also kept in the collection. Mark Grayson, a spokesman for Pharmaceutical Researchers and Manufacturers of America, the drug-industry lobbying group, described the Indian project as &quot;a solution in search of a problem.&quot; He said &quot;there is no evidence of bio- piracy,&quot; noting that most modern drugs are developed from chemicals with the aid of computers, rather than from natural substances. [http://www.thestandard.com.hk/news_detail.asp?pp_cat=20&amp;art_id=9484&amp;sid=6178209&amp;con_type=1&amp;d_str=20060110]

== Research costs and benefit sharing ==

Some companies argue that under-developed countries are themselves guilty of piracy. They believe that the southern countries do not have adequate and efficient intellectual property protection laws, and say they are losing millions of dollars per year because of lack of respect of patents. These companies have been applying pressure for the strengthening of intellectual property issues within the [[World Trade Organization|WTO]].

Companies say access to biological resources allows them to develop new products that could help solve food and health essential issues. They also argue that research, development and commercialisation authorisations have a cost that must be balanced by the protection of the resulting product. Patents offer this much needed revenue and favour innovation.
&lt;!-- &quot;Patents offer this much needed revenue&quot; is a little ambiguous. If the parts of speech are &quot;noun, verb, pronoun, adverb, participle, noun&quot;, then hyphenating &quot;much needed&quot; to &quot;much-needed&quot; can avoid ambiguity. If the parts of speech are &quot;noun, verb, adverb, adjective, participle, noun&quot;, then changing &quot;this much&quot; to &quot;this amount of&quot; can avoid ambiguity. --&gt;
One of the solutions suggested to solve this North-South disagreement was to define bilateral contracts between source country and pharmaceutical or seed companies.  These contracts of bioprospecting lay down the rules of benefit sharing, and can potentially bring substantial royalties to southern countries.

== Ownership of plants, animals, and human genes ==

The concept of biopiracy assumes that it is a [[natural right]] to own plants, animals and human genes.  For many indigenous people, [[nature]] and [[culture]] are indissociable.  [[Natural resource|Resources]] belong to the community, private property has no meaning. They argue that what is &quot;wrong&quot; is not so much the appropriation of somebody else's property, but rather the idea that natural resources, which should stay public, can be privatized.

In the [[United States]], the [[patent law]] has long protected the ability to protect &quot;isolated and purified&quot; compounds.  In [[1873]], [[Louis Pasteur]] patented a &quot;yeast&quot; which was &quot;free from disease&quot; (see patent #141072). US courts have upheld patents on biological substances like [[adrenaline]], [[chemical]]s, and even basic [[Chemical_element|element]]s.  

Patents covering biological inventions have been treated similarly. Importantly, however, the [[United States Patent and Trademark Office]] (USPTO) notes, &quot;A patent on a gene covers the isolated and purified gene but does not cover the gene as it occurs in nature.&quot;[http://www.uspto.gov/web/offices/com/sol/notices/utilexmguide.pdf]

== Preventing biopiracy ==

Preventing biopiracy is difficult for several reasons:
* Enforcing extra-[[jurisdiction]]al bilateral contracts is difficult.  Moreover, such contracts may not provide a [[fair trade]]. 
* There is a lack of awareness of a product's potential market value.
* Only a few of the harvested samples become new and profitable product
&lt;!-- I don't know what this means * lost ownership in case of genetic modification --&gt;
&lt;!-- Again, incoherent statement * majority of concerned species to be found in several countries at the same time, thus preventing some of them from taking advantage of the product, or diluting the benefits for all --&gt;
* Collective knowledge is not easily protected within [[intellectual property]].  Such knowledge can only be protected as [[trade secret]] or [[copyright]].
&lt;!-- blah, blah, blah * Finally, most bioprospecting is made by directly using the genetic resources stored in [[seed bank]]s. --&gt;

Some southern countries instead have tried: 
* documenting [[traditional knowledge]] to create [[prior art]] to defeat a patent;
* implementing registration and innovation system;
* reducing the cost of patenting for locals;
* developing of a [[sui generis]] system for protecting knowledge;
* increasing funding into the indigenous research programs;
* creating alliances with other source-countries for the protection of their biodiversity.

&lt;!-- This is not really on-point
In 1993, 500,000 Indian farmers demonstrated against the [[GATT|General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade]] and for the banning of genetically modified wheat. In a [[Charter of farmers' rights]], they stated their wish to protect their right to produce, reproduce and modify seeds and plants.  They never got their way. ---&gt;

== Advocacy ==

The issue of biopiracy is mostly raised by under-developed biodiversity-rich countries (e.g. [[India]], [[Brazil]], and [[Malaysia]], among others) and by some [[Non-governmental organization|NGO]]s (e.g., GRAIN, ETC (previously RAFI) and Third World Network), and is not always acknowledged by corporations and governments.

The issue of biopiracy is not new. In her book ''Biopiracy: The Plunder of Nature and Knowledge'', Third World environmentalist [[Vandana Shiva]] describes the way the [[natural capital]] of indigenous people (as applying to land, to labour and to knowledge), was converted under [[colonialism]]. She argues that since all lands have already been colonized by northern countries, northern corporations are now trying to colonize life itself, plants, micro-organisms, animals, and even human organs, cells, and genes.

Indeed, a controversial case of biopiracy was reported on human genes of a tribal community reported to be resistant to malaria and leprosy (Shiva).

According to some, biopiracy is not only happening at the expense of Third World farmers, but also at the expense of Northern farmers.  Some American and European farmers consider themselves to be compelled with contracts limiting their independence.

&lt;!-- not on-point
Some supporters of [[software piracy]] argue that outlawing the piracy of software created a  [[slippery slope]] which led to biopiracy and would probably lead to the monopolization and exploitation of a [[panacea]]. --&gt;


