  <page>
    <title>California Central Valley</title>
    <id>82140</id>
    <revision>
      <id>35511710</id>
      <timestamp>2006-01-17T06:52:31Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Ombudsman</username>
        <id>135955</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>/* Economy */ bypass redirect</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">{{about|the Californian geographic feature|the Central Valley of Costa Rica|[[Costa Rican Central Valley | Valle Central]]}}

[[Image:Wpdms shdrlfi020l california central valley.jpg|300px|thumbnail|right|The California Central Valley]]
[[Image:Californiacentralvalley.jpg|300px|thumb|right|Part of the Valley as seen from overhead]]
[[Image:Centralvalleyroad.jpg|300px|thumb|right|A typical Central Valley scene at ground level]]
The '''California Central Valley''' is a large, flat valley that dominates the central portion of the state of [[California]].  

Bounded by the [[Cascade Range]] to the north, the [[Sierra Nevada (US)|Sierra Nevada]] to the east, the [[Tehachapi Mountains]] to the south, and the [[Pacific Coast Ranges|Coast Range]] and [[San Francisco Bay]] to the west, this valley is a vast agricultural region drained by the [[Sacramento River|Sacramento]] and [[San Joaquin River|San Joaquin]] rivers.  The northern area (north of the [[Mokelumne River]] and the [[Sacramento River Delta|Sacramento-San Joaquin River Delta]]) is called the [[Sacramento Valley]]; the southern area is called the [[San Joaquin Valley]]. 

Nineteen counties are commonly associated with the Central Valley stretching for nearly 400 miles from north to south.  The area is further subdivided into four regions:  North Sacramento Valley ([[Shasta County, California|Shasta]], [[Tehama County, California|Tehama]], [[Glenn County, California|Glenn]], [[Butte County, California|Butte]], [[Colusa County, California|Colusa]]), Sacramento Metro ([[Sacramento County, California|Sacramento]], [[El Dorado County, California|El Dorado]], [[Sutter County, California|Sutter]], [[Yuba County, California|Yuba]], [[Yolo County, California|Yolo]], [[Placer County, California|Placer]]), North San Joaquin ([[San Joaquin County, California|San Joaquin]], [[Stanislaus County, California|Stanislaus]], [[Merced County, California|Merced]]), and South San Joaquin ([[Madera County, California|Madera]], [[Fresno County, California|Fresno]], [[Kings County, California|Kings]], [[Tulare County, California|Tulare]]).  [[Fresno, California|Fresno]] is the largest city followed closely by the state capital, [[Sacramento, California|Sacramento]].  [[Bakersfield, California|Bakersfield]], [[Stockton, California|Stockton]], and [[Modesto, California|Modesto]] are the other large cities in the region.  About 5.7 million people live in the Central Valley today.  

The Valley is renowned for its extreme flatness, in contrast to the rugged hills that are typical of most of California's terrain.  It is thought to have originated as the floor of an inland sea, when sea levels were much higher than they are today.

The Valley is also known for its hot weather in the summer and the thick [[tule fog]] in the winter.

==Rivers==
The [[Sacramento River|Sacramento]] and [[San Joaquin River]] flow through the northern and southern halves of the valley, respectively.  Major tributaries of these two rivers include:

===Sacramento Valley===
*[[Feather River]]
*[[American River]]

===San Joaquin Valley===
*[[Cosumnes River]]
*[[Mokelumne River]]
*[[Stanislaus River]]
*[[Tuolumne River]]
*[[Merced River]]
*[[Chowchilla River]]
*[[San Joaquin River]]
*[[Kings River (California)|Kings River]]
*[[Kern River]]

==Major cities==

Major cities in the Central Valley include [[Redding, California|Redding]], [[Chico, California|Chico]], [[Sacramento, California|Sacramento]] (California state capital and the Valley's largest metropolitan area), [[Stockton, California|Stockton]], [[Fresno, California|Fresno]] (largest city proper in the Valley), [[Modesto, California|Modesto]], [[Visalia, California|Visalia]]  and [[Bakersfield, California|Bakersfield]].

==Culture and politics==
Culturally and politically, the Central Valley is very different from the rest of California.  While the huge coastal cities are well-known for their liberal politics, the Valley (and most other rural areas) are quite conservative.  Some enclaves, such as Chico and Davis, are more generally liberal than the rest of the Valley, but their surrounding counties are typically conservative.  The city of Sacramento is also more liberal than the Valley norm, but its surrounding suburbs, particularly in Placer and El Dorado Counties, are conservative.

==Economy==
The [[Central Valley Project]] was formed in [[1935]] to redistribute and store water for agricultural and municipal purposes with dams and canals.  The Central Valley has become famous around the world as the home of California's giant [[agriculture]] industry; the fertile soil is also thought to have originated from the days when the valley was a sea floor.  There are also some large cattle and livestock ranches.

Because of its physical remoteness, the Valley is also home to many [[California Department of Corrections and Rehabilitation|California Department of Corrections]] institutions.

==Social issues==
Since the [[1980s]], Bakersfield, Fresno, [[Visalia, California|Visalia]], [[Tracy, California|Tracy]] and [[Modesto, California|Modesto]] have exploded in both size and population, as housing values along the coast reached astronomical values and drove many California residents inland in search of the most important component of the American dream -- homeownership.  Many people from the [[San Francisco Bay Area]] have moved out to more rural areas in search of affordable housing, clogging the roads between their Valley homes and their Bay Area jobs.

Unfortunately, these cities (along with Sacramento) have been confronted by big city problems including [[violent crime]], [[drug trafficking]], [[organized crime]], [[traffic congestion]], and [[air pollution]].  The San Joaquin Valley now has the worst air quality in California (and the highest [[asthma]] rates), and its cities are subject to the most stringent anti-pollution measures in the state.  Some people also worry that the rapidly expanding [[urban sprawl]] will completely displace agriculture (as happened in the [[Santa Clara Valley|Santa Clara]] and [[San Fernando Valley]]s).

==External links==
* [http://www.greatvalley.org/ Great Valley Center]
* [http://www.valleyvision.org/ Valley Vision]
* [http://www.terragalleria.com/california/california.central-valley.html Photos of the Central Valley - Terra Galleria]

{{California}}

[[Category:Geologic provinces of California]]
[[Category:Valleys of California|Central Valley, California]]

[[ja:カリフォルニアセントラルヴァレー]]</text>
    </revision>
