  <page>
    <title>Edward Frederic Benson</title>
    <id>82142</id>
    <revision>
      <id>38001997</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-03T14:41:13Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Grstain</username>
        <id>74943</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Margaret Rumer Godden</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Edward Frederic Benson''' ([[July 24]], [[1867]] &amp;ndash; [[February 29]], [[1940]]) was an [[England|English]] novelist, biographer, memoirist and short story writer, known professionally as '''E.F. Benson'''.  His friends called him Fred.

E.F. Benson was born at [[Wellington College (Berkshire)|Wellington College]] in Berkshire, the fifth child of the headmaster, [[Edward White Benson]] (later [[Archbishop of Canterbury]]), and [[Mary Sidgwick Benson]].  He was a brother of [[A. C. Benson|Arthur Christopher Benson]], who wrote the words to ''[[Land of Hope and Glory]]'', and [[Robert Hugh Benson]], author of several novels and Catholic [[apologetic]] works.

Despite starting his novel writing career with the (then) fashionably controversial ''Dodo'', and following it with a variety of satire and romantic melodrama, Benson is principally known for his [[Mapp and Lucia]] series, consisting of six novels and three short stories about Emmeline &quot;Lucia&quot; Lucas and Elizabeth Mapp.  The novels are:  ''Queen Lucia'', ''Lucia in London'', ''Miss Mapp'' (including the short story ''The Male Impersonator''), ''Mapp and Lucia'', ''Lucia's Progress'' (published as ''The Worshipful Lucia'' in the [[US]]) and ''Trouble for Lucia''.   

[[image:P1000593.jpg|thumb|right|260px|Lamb House, home of E.F. Benson and model for &quot;Mallards&quot; in the Lucia series]]

The principal setting of four of the books is a town called Tilling, which is recognizably based on [[Rye, East Sussex]], where Benson lived for many years and served as Mayor.  Benson's home, [[Lamb House]], served as the model for Mallards, Lucia's home in some of the Tilling series. Lamb House was earlier the home of [[Henry James]], and later of [[Margaret Rumer Godden]].

In London, Benson also lived at 395 [[Oxford Street]], [[London postal district|W1]] (now the branch of Russell &amp; Bromley just west of [[Bond Street tube station|Bond Street Underground Station]]), 102 [[Oakley Street]], [[London postal district|SW3]], and 25 [[Brompton Square]], [[London postal district|SW3]], where much of the action of ''Lucia in London'' takes place and where [[English Heritage]] placed a Blue Plaque in [[1994]].

The last three books were serialized by [[London Weekend Television]] for the fledgling [[Channel 4]] in 1985–6 under the series title &quot;Mapp and Lucia.&quot; and starring [[Prunella Scales]], [[Geraldine McEwan]] and [[Nigel Hawthorne]]; the first three have been adapted for [[BBC Radio 4]] by both [[Aubrey Woods]] and (most recently) [[Ned Sherrin]]. 


Benson was also known as a writer of [[ghost stories]], which frequently appear in collections, and of a series of biographies/autobiographies and memoirs, including one of [[Charlotte Brontë]].  His last book, completed shortly before his death, was a memoir entitled ''As We Were''.

A critical essay on Benson's ghost stories appears in [[S. T. Joshi]]'s book ''The Evolution of the Weird Tale'' (2004).

He died in 1940 of throat cancer in [[University College Hospital]], [[London]].

==External links==
*[http://www.efbenson.co.uk/ E.F. Benson website]
*{{gutenberg author|id=E._F._Benson|name=E. F. Benson}}
*[http://www.rye-tourism.co.uk/efbenson/index.htm Brief Benson biography]
*[http://www.opensquares.org/pages/square/9.html Brompton Square history]
*[http://www.londontown.com/LondonStreets/brompton_square_4c2.html Brompton Square map]
*[http://www.rye-tourism.co.uk/ Rye website]
*[http://www.tillingsociety.com/ The Tilling Society]
*[http://www.tillinggathering.com/ The Friends of Tilling]
*[http://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/main/w-vh/w-visits/w-findaplace/w-lambhouse/ National Trust: Lamb House]

[[Category:1867 births|Benson, Edward Frederic]]
[[Category:1940 deaths|Benson, Edward Frederic]]
[[Category:English novelists|Benson, Edward Frederic]]
[[Category:English horror writers|Benson, Edward Frederic]]</text>
