  <page>
    <title>Hermaphroditus</title>
    <id>82161</id>
    <revision>
      <id>37931493</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-03T01:18:26Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Cmdrjameson</username>
        <id>101935</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>sp: refered→referred</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Salmacis and Hermaphroditus.jpg|thumb|''Salmacis and Hermaphroditus'' by [[Bartholomeus Spranger]] (c. [[1598]])]]
In [[Greek mythology]], '''Hermaphroditus''' (or '''Hermaphroditos''') was a child of [[Aphrodite]] and [[Hermes]].  He was born a remarkably handsome boy but was transformed into a [[hermaphrodite]] (one of only two found in Greek mythology, along with [[Tiresias]]), by the [[nymph]] [[Salmacis]].  His only attestation is in Book IV of [[Ovid]]'s ''[[Metamorphoses (poem)|Metamorphoses]]''.

Hermaphroditus' name is derived from those of his parents, [[Aphrodite]] and [[Hermes]]. All three of these gods figure largely into the Greek tradition of fertility gods and all possess distinctly sexual overtones. Sometimes, Hermaphroditus is referred to as '''Aphroditus'''.  Half-siblings of Hermaphroditus include the phallic god [[Priapus]] and the youthful god of desire [[Eros (mythology)|Eros]]. 

== Story ==

Hermaphroditus was raised by nymphs on [[Mount Ida]], a sacred mountain in [[Phrygia]].  At the age of fifteen, he grew bored of his surroundings and traveled the cities of [[Lycia]] and [[Caria]].  It was in the woods of Caria that he encountered [[Salmacis]] the [[Naiads|Naiad]] in her pool.  She is overcome by lust for the boy, and tries to seduce him, but is rejected.  When he thinks her to be gone, Hermaphroditus undresses and enters the waters of the empty pool.  Salmacis springs out from behind a tree and jumps into the pool. She wraps herself around the boy, forcibly kissing him and touching his breast.  While he struggles, she calls out to the [[greek gods|gods]] in prayer that they should never part.  Her wish is granted, and their bodies blend into one [[intersex|intersexual]] form.  Hermaphroditus, in his grief, makes his own prayer: cursing the pool so that any other who bathes within it shall be transformed as well.

[[Category:Greek mythological people]]

[[de:Hermaphroditos]]
[[fr:Hermaphrodite]]
[[it:Ermafrodito]]
[[he:הרמפרודיטוס]]
[[lt:Hermafroditas (mitologija)]]
[[hu:Hermaphroditosz]]
[[nl:Hermaphroditus]]
[[pl:Hermafrodyta]]
[[pt:Hermafrodito]]
[[uk:Гермафродит (міфологія)]]</text>
    </revision>
