  <page>
    <title>Lace card</title>
    <id>82192</id>
    <revision>
      <id>31762929</id>
      <timestamp>2005-12-17T20:12:46Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Keenan Pepper</username>
        <id>124371</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>[[Categpry:Practical jokes]]</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:IBM lace card.jpg|right|thumb|300px|A lace card from the early [[1970s]].]]

A '''lace card''' is a [[punch card]] with all holes punched (also called a ''whoopee card,'' ''ventilator card'' or ''IBM doily''). Card readers tended to jam when they got to one of these, as the resulting card had too little structural strength to avoid buckling inside the mechanism. Card punches could also jam trying to produce these things owing to power-supply problems. When some practical joker fed a lace card through the reader, you needed to clear the jam with a 'card knife'. 

A more modern equivalent is a [[black fax]].

{{FOLDOC}}

[[Category:History of computing]]
[[Category:Practical jokes]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Church of the Brethren</title>
    <id>82193</id>
    <revision>
      <id>39377510</id>
      <timestamp>2006-02-12T19:44:39Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>Veledan</username>
        <id>306701</id>
      </contributor>
      <minor />
      <comment>communion --&gt; Eucharist. Disambiguation link repair ([[Wikipedia:Disambiguation pages with links|You can help!]])</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Church of the Brethren''' was organized by [[Alexander Mack]], a [[miller]], in [[Schwarzenau]], [[Germany]], in [[1708]].  The first church was established in America in [[1723]]. These churches became commonly known as '''German Baptist Brethren'''. It is a [[Protestant]], [[Anabaptist]] Church.  The denomination holds the New Testament as its only [[creed]]. Historically the church has taken a strong stance for [[non-resistance]] or [[pacifism]]. Distinctive practices include [[believers baptism]] by trine immersion, a three-fold Love Feast consisting of [[feet washing]], a fellowship meal, and [[Eucharist|communion]], anointing for healing, and the [[holy kiss]].

The ''Church of the Brethren'' represents the largest body descending from Mack's ''[[Schwarzenau Brethren]]'' church. The ''German Baptist Brethren'' suffered major division in the early [[1880s]], creating the [[Old German Baptist Brethren]], the [[The Brethren Church|Brethren Church]], and the majority adopting the name ''Church of the Brethren'' in [[1908]]. It had 131,200 members in about 1100 congregations and fellowships in [[2004]]. There are six liberal arts colleges and one seminary ([[Bethany Theological Seminary]] in [[Richmond, Indiana]]) related to the Church of the Brethren. General offices and the ''[[Brethren Press]]'' are located in [[Elgin, Illinois]].

In [[1948]] the ''Church of the Brethren'' joined the [[World Council of Churches]] as a charter member, and was a forming member of the [[National Council of Churches]] in [[1950]].

==External links==

Related groups that trace their beginnings to the Schwarzenau Brethren are:

*[http://www.brethrenchurch.org The Brethren Church]
*[http://www.fgbc.org The Fellowship of Grace Brethren Churches]
*[http://my.raex.com/~ogbc/CGBCI Conservative Grace Brethren Churches, International]
*[http://www.dunkardbrethrenchurch.com The Dunkard Brethren Church]

Brethren-related websites:

*[http://www.brethren.org The official homepage of the Church of the Brethren]
*[http://www.cob-net.org Church of the Brethren] - ''unofficial'' Web Site
*[http://www.cob-net.org/timeline.htm Church of the Brethren Timeline]
*[http://www.cob-net.org/webdirectory.htm Brethren Web Directory] (with links to all Churches of the Brethren with websites)
*[http://www.brethren.org/genbd/global_mission/Nigeria/NigeriaHistory.html Brethren History in Nigeria]
*[http://www.brfwitness.org Brethren Revival Fellowship] (a conservative movement within and loyal to the Church of the Brethren)

Brethren-related Liberal Arts Colleges:

*[http://www.manchester.edu/ Manchester College] in North Manchester, Indiana
*[http://www.juniata.edu/ Juniata College] in Huntingdon, Pennsylvania
*[http://www.etown.edu/ Elizabethtown College] in Elizabethtown, Pennsylvania
*[http://www.bridgewater.edu/ Bridgewater College] in Bridgewater, Virginia
*[http://www.mcpherson.edu/ McPherson College] in McPherson, Kansas
*[http://www.ulv.edu/ University of La Verne] in La Verne, California

==References==
*''Encyclopedia of American Religions'', J. Gordon Melton, editor
*''Handbook of Denominations'', by Frank S. Mead, Samuel S. Hill, and Craig D. Atwood
*''Profiles in Belief: the Religious Bodies of the United States and Canada'', by Arthur Carl Piepkorn
*''Religious Congregations &amp; Membership in the United States (2000)'', Glenmary Research Center
[[Category:Anabaptism]]
[[Category:Christian denominations]]
[[Category:Pacifism]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
