|-
|
{| border=&quot;1&quot; cellpadding=&quot;2&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; align=&quot;right&quot; width=&quot;400&quot; style=&quot;margin:10px&quot;
|- align=&quot;center&quot;
!colspan=&quot;2&quot; cellspacing=&quot;0&quot; cellpadding=&quot;2&quot; bgcolor=&quot;#skyblue&quot;|Instruments
|-
|'''Lyman-Alpha Telescope''' || to scan earth to study hydrogen constituent of exosphere
|-
|'''Magnetometer''' || to measure magnetic field in interplanetary space
|}
|}
'''Ranger 1''' was a spacecraft in the [[Ranger program]] whose primary mission was to test the performance of those functions and parts necessary for carrying out subsequent lunar and planetary missions. A secondary objective was to study the nature of particles and fields in interplanetary space.

== Spacecraft design ==

The spacecraft was of the Ranger Block 1 design and consisted of a hexagonal base 1.5 [[meter|m]] across upon which was mounted a cone-shaped 4 m high tower of aluminum struts and braces. Two solar panel wings measuring 5.2 m from tip to tip extended from the base. A high-gain directional dish antenna was attached to the bottom of the base. Spacecraft experiments and other equipment were mounted on the base and tower. Instruments aboard the spacecraft included a Lyman-alpha telescope, a [[rubidium-vapor]] [[magnetometer]], electrostatic analyzers, medium-energy range particle detectors, two triple coincidence telescopes, a cosmic-ray integrating [[ionization chamber]], cosmic dust detectors, and solar X-ray [[scintillation counter]]s.

The communications system included the high gain antenna and an omni-directional medium gain antenna and two transmitters, one at 960.1 [[Megahertz|MHz]] with 0.25 [[Watt|W]] power output and the other at 960.05 MHz with 3 W power output. Power was to be furnished by 8680 solar cells on the two panels, a 57 kg silver-zinc battery, and smaller batteries on some of the experiments. Attitude control was provided by a solid-state timing controller, [[Sun]] and [[Earth]] sensors, and pitch and roll jets. The temperature was controlled passively by gold plating, white paint, and polished aluminum surfaces.

== Mission ==

The Ranger 1 spacecraft was designed to go into an Earth parking orbit and then into a 60,000 by 1,100,000 km Earth orbit to test systems and strategies for future lunar missions. Ranger 1 was launched into the Earth parking orbit as planned, but the Agena B failed to restart to put it into the higher trajectory, so when Ranger 1 separated from the Agena stage it went into a low Earth orbit and began tumbling. The satellite re-entered Earth's atmosphere on [[August 30]], [[1961]]. Ranger 1 was partially successful, much of the primary objective of flight testing the equipment was accomplished but little scientific data was returned.

==External link==
*[http://ntrs.nasa.gov/archive/nasa/casi.ntrs.nasa.gov/19780007206_1978007206.pdf Lunar impact: A history of Project Ranger (PDF) 1977]

{{Ranger program | before=Start of program | after=[[Ranger 2]] }}

[[Category:Ranger program|1]]

[[nl:Ranger 1]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
  <page>
    <title>Boston Massacre</title>
    <id>82254</id>
    <revision>
      <id>41919004</id>
      <timestamp>2006-03-02T16:48:24Z</timestamp>
      <contributor>
        <username>No Guru</username>
        <id>44087</id>
      </contributor>
      <comment>Boston massacre could refer to the 2004 series but not to the '78 one as Boston did not play in this worls series</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Boston Massacre.jpg|300px|right|thumb|Engraving by Paul Revere]]
:''Boston Massacre could also refer to the [[2004 World Series]].''
The '''Boston Massacre''' was an event that occurred on a snowy Monday night, [[March 5]], [[1770]] and helped eventually spark the [[Revolutionary War]].  Tensions caused by the [[military occupation]] of [[Boston]] were greatly increased after [[soldier]]s fired into a rioting crowd of [[civilian]]s. [[John Adams]] later said that on the night of  the Boston Massacre, the foundation of independent America was laid.


The incident began when a young wigmaker's apprentice named Edward Garrick approached a customs officer on the night [[March 5]] in King Street with a request for a payment due to his master. When the apprentice became quite vocal in his demands, a [[Kingdom of Great Britain|British]] sentry, Private White, left his post outside the customs house to club the boy for his impudence. The angry Garrick returned soon after with a group of colonists who pelted White with [[snowball]]s, ice, and trash.

The commotion brought out the Officer of the Day, [[Thomas Preston (Soldier)|Captain Thomas Preston]], who came to the White's aid with a corporal and eight private soldiers of the [[29th Regiment of Foot]]. The mob grew in size and continued throwing stones, sticks, and chunks of ice at the British soldiers.
 
In the midst of the commotion, Captain Preston's order of &quot;Don't Fire&quot; was heard. One soldier, Private Hugh Montgomery, was struck down onto the ground from a club, which led to him &quot;accidentally&quot; firing his musket into the air. (He later admitted to firing deliberately into the mob.) This led to a panic from the other soldiers and they began firing into the crowd. Three Americans, Samuel Gray, James Caldwell, and a [[mulatto]], [[Crispus Attucks]], died instantly and two others died later.  Samuel Maverick died the next day and Patrick Carr about a week later. Six more were injured.  The event was depicted by a Boston silversmith and [[engraving|engraver]] named [[Paul Revere]] who lived nearby. Revere incorrectly showed the event as occurring at close quarters, with no [[snow]] on the ground, with Crispus Attucks as being a white man, and with the British commander standing behind his troops. The sign over the Customs House was changed to read &quot;Butcher's Hall.&quot;

Christian Remick, who colored the engraving, incorrectly depicted the event as happening during the day time by adding a bright blue sky (although a quarter [[moon]] was present).

[[Image:boston_massacre_grave_20040930_105414_1.627x1068.jpg|thumb|200px|left|Boston Massacre grave marker]]

The soldiers involved were tried in a civilian court with a jury composed of colonials and with [[John Adams]] and [[Josiah Quincy II]] acting as the defense [[attorney at law|attorneys]]. Six of the soldiers were found not guilty, and two more -- the only two proven to have double loaded their muskets, Hugh Montgomery and Matthew Killroy, were found guilty of [[manslaughter]], which was punished by branding their thumbs.  The [[commissioned officer|officer]], Captain Thomas Preston, was acquitted when the [[jury]] was unable to determine whether he had in fact ordered the troops to fire. The jury's decisions suggest that they believed that the soldiers had clearly been provoked and that their use of force was not unreasonable. 

[[Samuel Adams]] dubbed the event &quot;''the Boston Massacre''&quot; and used it for [[propaganda]] purposes. He argued that the significance lay not in the small number of [[death]]s, but in the involvement of ordinary working class Americans in an important [[revolution]]ary event. Following the funerals of the five men, which was the largest gathering ever on the [[North America]]n [[continent]] at that time, Bostonians gathered at the [[Old North Church]] to demand that the British soldiers be removed from the city.

[[bs:Bostonski pokolj]]
[[eo:Bostona Masakro]]
[[fr:Massacre de Boston]]
[[he:טבח בוסטון]]
[[hu:Bostoni sortűz]]
[[nl:Bloedbad van Boston]]
[[pl:Masakra bostońska]]

[[Category:American Revolution|Boston Massacre]]</text>
    </revision>
  </page>
