      <comment>Reverted edits by [[Special:Contributions/Shawna browning|Shawna browning]] ([[User talk:Shawna browning|talk]]) to last version by Antandrus</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">A '''Tsarina''' ([[Russian language|Russian]] and [[Bulgarian language|Bulgarian]]: цари́ца, '''tsaritsa'''), also spelled '''czarina''', or '''czaritsa''',  was the title of [[Tsar]]'s wife or a female [[autocrat]]ic  ruler ([[monarch]]) of [[Russia]] or [[Bulgaria]]. Since [[1721]], the official titles of the Russian male and female monarchs were [[Emperor]] and [[Empress]], respectively.

Several Tsarinas were the rulers of Russia including [[Catherine I of Russia|Catherine I]], [[Catherine the Great|Catherine the Great]] and [[Elizabeth of Russia|Elizabeth I]] . 

Others who gained the title by marrying a Tsar were [[Princess Maria Louisa of Baden|Elizabeth Alexeevna]], Alexandra Fyodorovna ([[Charlotte of Prussia]]), Maria Alexandrovna (Marie of Hesse-Rhein), [[Dagmar of Denmark|Marie Romanova]] and [[Eleonore, Princess Reuss-Köstritz]], who became Tsarina of Bulgaria following her marriage to Tsar [[Ferdinand of Bulgaria|Ferdinand]]. 

The last Russian Tsarina was [[Princess]] [[Alexandra of Hesse|Alix of Hesse]] who was married to [[Nicholas II of Russia]].  The last Bulgarian Tsarina was [[Giovanna of Bulgaria]], the wife of Tsar [[Boris III of Bulgaria]]. 


{{Russian-history-stub}}
{{bulgaria-stub}}

[[Category:Imperial Russia]]
[[Category:Lists of office-holders|Russia, Tsaritsa]]
[[Category:Russian leaders]]
[[Category:Titles]]</text>
