      </contributor>
      <comment>/* See also */</comment>
      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:Inductor.jpg|thumb|150px|An [[inductor]].]]
The '''henry''' (symbol: H) is the [[SI]] unit of [[inductance]]. It is named after the [[United States|American]] scientist [[Joseph Henry]].

==Definition==
If the rate of change of [[Current (electricity)|current]] in a circuit is one [[ampere]] per [[second]] and the resulting [[electromotive force]] is one [[volt]], then the inductance of the circuit is one henry.

1 H = Wb/A = 1 m&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;·kg·s&lt;sup&gt;–2&lt;/sup&gt;·A&lt;sup&gt;–2&lt;/sup&gt; = 1 V·s/A

==SI multiples==
{{SI multiples|unit=henry|symbol=H}}



==See also==
* [[Impedance]]



[[Category:SI derived units]]
[[Category:Units of electrical inductance]]

